\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Asia Stock Markets End Lower; Southeast Asia Slump Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2013\par 12:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Abhrajit Gangopadhyay, Mia Lamar and Nopparat Chaichalearmmongkol \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets across Asia slumped Tuesday, sending Indonesian stocks tumbling for a second day and the Indian rupee to a fresh record low, as the end of U.S. monetary easing draws attention to slowing growth in the region and rising debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The most dramatic moves were in Southeast Asia, with Indonesian stocks falling 9% over the past two days and 20% from a high in May. Thailand shed 2% Tuesday and touched its weakest this year, while the turmoil caught up Malaysia's ringgit, which hit a three-year low.\par \par \uc2 The broad selloff comes just before the release Wednesday of the Federal Reserve's June policy-meeting minutes, which may give more clues to how close the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to winding down its stimulus program that has pumped the global economy full of cheap money.\par \par \uc2 Investors are fleeing emerging markets as rising Treasury yields lessen the appeal of riskier assets such as Indonesia, India and Thailand. That's left the MSCI Asia ex-Japan index down about 6% this year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, signs are growing that Asia's economy is losing its shine. Thailand said this week it has entered a recession, while Indonesia reported its trade imbalance worsened with its current-account balance widening in the second quarter as the economy sputters along at its slowest pace in three years.\par \par \uc2 "There is massive outflow of funds from the emerging markets" around the world, said Choo Swee Kee, chief investment officer at TA Investment Management, who manages an equivalent of 700 million ringgit ($213 million) in Malaysian assets.\par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 1% to 31.74 baht per dollar, its weakest level in more than a year. Malaysia's shares fell as much as 2%, with the benchmark up just 3.5% this year compared to a 10% rally over the course of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said Tuesday that pressure on Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain high because of its trade imbalance and heavy ownership of the country's bonds by foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Funds have been drifting out of emerging markets and into developed markets as they are showing signs of recovery, said Komsorn Prakopphol, an analyst at \uc2 Tisco Financial Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. jobless claims are at their lowest level in nearly six years, helping push up the 10-year benchmark Treasury yield to within striking distance of 3%--a two-year high. Meanwhile Europe's economy, which has been battered over the past few years because of a debt crisis, is starting to turn up.\par \par \uc2 The selloff in Asia's emerging markets comes after a heady stretch for the region that began building in force late last year. Thailand's benchmark index, for example, gained nearly 30% between mid-November and its May high, while Indonesian shares rallied 20%.\par \par \uc2 "If there's a redeeming feature to the current selloff, it's that investors are the most concerned about the emerging markets" which are showing signs of weakness, said Nicholas Spiro, managing director of Spiro Sovereign Strategy in London. "Unfortunately, it took a sudden rise in U.S. interest rates to force investors to pay more attention to country-specific risk."\par \par \uc2 Some signs are emerging, too, that Asian governments are starting to fight back.\par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the ringgit's slide. \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't comment on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but has said that when it takes actions, they are aimed only at reducing volatility in the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release second-quarter economic growth and balance of payment data Wednesday, with some investors worried a shrinking trade surplus will squeeze Malaysia's current-account surplus and push it into deficit territory for the first time since 1997.\par \par \uc2 India banned duty-free imports of television sets by individuals late Monday in its latest effort to narrow the country's current-account deficit, which traders blame for the rupee's slide. Still, that didn't stop the rupee's woes Tuesday, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting a record low and stocks falling again.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street bank \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  on Tuesday downgraded the outlook for Indian stocks to neutral--or hold--from buy, saying the falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had changed the calculations for India's potential growth.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Nikkei Stock Average slid 2.6%, and the yen strengthened to Y97.11 to the U.S. dollar versus Y97.57 late Monday in New York. Chinese stocks were also stuck in weak trade, with Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index down 2.2% and the mainland's Shanghai Composite Index off 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Abhrajit Gangopadhyay at abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com, Mia Lamar at mia.lamar@wsj.com and Nopparat Chaichalearmmongkol and noppart.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 20, 2013 06:24 ET (10:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130820e98k002g6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
