\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/MYR Higher; May Rise Toward 3.3000\b0\par\par\uc2 1,633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2013\par 11:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR is at 3.2840 versus 3.2770, the highest since June 2010, reflecting strong investor buying interest and the greenback's strength against other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Concern over Malaysia's slowing economic growth and firm USD bids may lift the pair toward 3.3000 in the near term, a local trader says, although he also warns that gains may be capped on possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent sharp declines in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 USD/TWD is higher, boosted by foreign investor buying while also reflecting the greenback's strength against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say two local traders. The pair is at 30.032 versus 30.002 late Friday in Asia. \uc2 Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected of buying around 29.960 to keep the pair in its target range, the traders say. They add that importers buying below 29.930 and the USD/KRW's rise to 1,115 lift the pair. The Taiwan dollar often tracks the won as South Korea is Taiwan's major export competitor. "With rising yields of U.S. Treasurys pushing down emerging market bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the greenback is still likely to rise further in the near term," a trader says. But the pair is unlikely to rise sharply as exporters' selling will likely intensify near 30.000, she says. The two traders tip the pair to trade in a 29.930-29.990 band Tuesday. Taiwan government bonds fall in thin volume, with the most-traded five-year bond yield at 1.1720% vs 1.1650% late Friday; a trader tips the yield to test resistance 1.2000% Tuesday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 as the daily Ichimoku Cloud caps 0.9241. The AUD/USD pair has turned away from an earlier probe of the Cloud resistance zone and is now lower. Failure to close above 0.9222 would also deny the upside potential that the Bollinger uptrend channel could impart to the Aussie. The presence of these two strong technical resistance levels suggests that AUD/USD could drift lower toward 0.9106, where the 20-day Bollinger mid lies. AUD/USD is now 0.9196.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar rises to a three-year high of 3.2835 against the Malaysia ringgit as the market braces for a withdrawal of U.S. quantitative easing. The ringgit is under pressure as analysts forecast a possible 2Q current account deficit Wednesday. It may continue weakening, the daily chart suggests. The greenback is rallying in anticipation of a possibly hawkish tone in the U.S. Federal Reserve's FOMC minutes. The minutes, to be released Wednesday, could affirm conviction that the Fed will begin tapering monetary stimulus next month. USD/MYR rises toward 3.3000, along the daily Bollinger uptrend channel that now supports at 3.2700. USD/MYR is now at 3.2830. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0559 GMT [Nikkei/Dow Jones] \uc2 Kawasaki Heavy Industries\uc2  (7012.TO) is up 1.9% at Y375 following a Nikkei report that the firm will team up with \uc2 Rolls-Royce\uc2  (RR.LN) to develop a new type of aircraft engine that is 30% more fuel efficient than conventional jet engines. The British engine manufacturer is leading the project and is discussing the engine's commercialization with aircraft manufacturer \uc2 Airbus SAS\uc2 , with \uc2 Kawasaki Heavy\uc2  expected to develop and supply a key control component, the paper says. "The arrangement likely won't help with short-term profits for \uc2 Kawasaki Heavy\uc2 , but the gradual penetration of such engines in the global market should eventually help to support its share price," says an analyst at a domestic brokerage. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0512 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Results from the Australian earnings period so far are supportive of the view that earnings for the market ex-resources have troughed, despite tough operating conditions in fiscal 2013, say Macquarie equities strategists. With about 1/3 of companies by market capitalization having reported, Macquarie analysts forecast aggregate EPS growth of 14.7% for all listed Australian companies, up from 13.4% last month. "While earnings growth remains weak, and the upswing in growth is uneven across the market, the tone of the results reflects the resolve to lift earnings through the increasingly intense focus on cost reductions," the strategists say in a report. They add that reports also suggest that negative momentum for resources earnings is also slowing, albeit mainly due to cost control. Mining services is now the only broad group of companies where reports suggest the balance of risks for the fiscal 2014 earnings outlook remains skewed to the downside, according to Macquarie. (david.rogers1@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/INR hit a new all-time high of 62.45 amid ongoing expectation that the U.S. Federal Reserve will, as soon as September, reduce its monthly purchase of government bonds. The rupee has depreciated close to 12.0% this year, and with this new record low, there is little basis for suggesting where the collapse might bottom. Psychological trading barriers such as the round-number of 63.00 to the dollar might be the next most obvious support level for the rupee. There has been little respite for the Indian rupee despite a recent slew of measures to correct the chronic current account deficit while simultaneously making it more expensive for onshore banks to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. Federal Reserve looks set to withdraw monetary stimulus in a gradual manner as soon as next month, adding to dollar-bullish pressures on the USD/INR. The pair is now at 62.26.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia's economic growth likely slowed sharply in April-June period, rising 4.7% from a year earlier, according to the median forecast of 15 economists in a \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  poll. This compares to the 4.1% on-year expansion in 1Q. For the full-year, economists expect 4.7% growth, slowing from 5.6% in 2012, the same survey shows. Economists say a slew of weak exports data likely hurt GDP growth in 2Q despite resilient domestic demand. "Malaysia still needs a stronger rebound in the global economy, particularly with the anticipated positive impact on commodity prices, for the economy to maintain its growth momentum," says OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi. Separately, DBS Group says exports may improve but still remain subdued due to "overhanging uncertainties" in the global economy in 2Q. The official data is due Wednesday at 1001 GMT. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZD/USD has drifted higher on the absence of any news and little to focus on before the release of FOMC meeting minutes late in the week, says \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions Corporate Dealing Manager Chris Hunter; "It's fairly light this week. There are a lot of second-tier and third-tier releases, so the Kiwi is likely to move sideways until we see some sentiment swings." The pair is at 0.8123 versus 0.8102 early this morning. Hunter puts support for the pair at 0.8085 with resistance at 0.8166. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0337 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Crude prices appear to be on the high side and once supply concerns ease, a correction could occur, says Lee Chen Hoay, an investment analyst at Phillip Futures. Traders remain focused on the upheaval in Egypt and the prospect of oil-supply disruptions due to its status as a major oil-transit point. However, he doesn't expect \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude to fall below $100 a barrel in the next few weeks. The weekly oil-stock data from the \uc2 Energy Information Administration\uc2  mid-week and HSBC China's manufacturing PMI for August Thursday could provide further cues. If EIA data show there has been a significant fall in weekly stocks at Cushing, Oklahoma -- a major crude oil trading hub -- the difference between \uc2 Nymex\uc2  and Brent crude prices could narrow further. September \uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude is at $107.43/bbl, down 3 cents, while October Brent is at $110.42/bbl, up 2 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] PREVIEW: Hong Kong May-July jobless rate likely steady at 3.3%, unchanged from April-June period, according to the median forecast of four economists polled by the Wall Street Journal. "It seems different environment in various sectors just cancel off with each other--we have a very tight labor market for the hotel and catering, general services etc. w1hile the financial sector is still struggling," says \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  senior strategist Frances Cheung. The government is scheduled to release the data at 0830 GMT Monday. (chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan's export orders in July likely fell for the sixth consecutive month due to soft global demand for the country's electronic products, say economists. A \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  survey of 10 economists tips a 1.5% on-year drop in July after an unexpected 3.5% decline in June. Economists say a slower-than-expected recovery in Europe continues to dent Taiwan's exports while consumption in U.S. and Chinese markets remains weak. "There's a chance the global economy may pick up slightly in the second half due to annual year-end sales, giving Taiwan's exports a slight boost. But unless China implements new stimulus measures soon, any rebound would be weak at best," says Lai Yi-sin, an economist at the Yuanta-Polaris Research Institute. The government is scheduled to release the data Tuesday at 0800 GMT. (jenny.hsu@wsj.com) [ 19-08-13 0913GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130819e98j0009o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
