\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/MYR Rises; 3.2500 Cap Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2013\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Dutch Newswire\par \uc2 RTDJDU\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/MYR rises to 3.2242 from 3.2065 late Friday in Asia, a level not seen since Oct. 3, 2011. "The ringgit is under further pressure after the one month USD/MYR NDFs hit the highest levels since June 24 amid talk of ringgit selling on bond outflows and selling from real money funds," a local trader says, although she notes that month-end settlements by exporters and cautiousness over potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Malaysia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could limit the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weakness. She tips a near-term cap for the pair at 3.2500. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 The USD/MYR rises to 3.2242 from 3.2065 late Friday in Asia, a level not seen since Oct. 3, 2011. "The ringgit is under further pressure after the one month USD/MYR NDFs hit the highest levels since June 24 amid talk of ringgit selling on bond outflows and selling from real money funds," a local trader says, although she notes that month-end settlements by exporters and cautiousness over potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could limit the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness. She tips a near-term cap for the pair at 3.2500. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW is lower at 1,110.5 vs 1,111.1 late Friday in Seoul on month-end export settlements. Still, the pair managed to bounce from the 1,108.7 intraday low with some offshore players buying the dollar, says a local bank trader. "We were busy executing sell orders throughout the day, but later in the session, some offshore players turned biddish," says the trader. They could be unwinding some of their short positions ahead of the Federal Reserve meeting later this week, the trader adds. The trader tips the pair to trade in a 1,105-1,114 band on Tuesday. (kanga.kong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW is lower at 1,110.5 vs 1,111.1 late Friday in Seoul on month-end export settlements. Still, the pair managed to bounce from the 1,108.7 intraday low with some offshore players buying the dollar, says a local bank trader. "We were busy executing sell orders throughout the day, but later in the session, some offshore players turned biddish," says the trader. They could be unwinding some of their short positions ahead of the Federal Reserve meeting later this week, the trader adds. The trader tips the pair to trade in a 1,105-1,114 band on Tuesday. (kanga.kong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 PREVIEW: Japan's industrial output likely fell 1.7% on-month in June, according to the median forecast of economists surveyed by Dow Jones and the Nikkei, as drops in auto production weigh on output. The fall comes after a revised 1.9% rise in May and marks the first drop since January. Still, the rising trend in industrial output is likely to continue, economists say. A corporate survey last month showed companies expect output to increase 3.3% on month in July. "Although we can't deny the possibility that production will come under pressure by a slowdown in overseas economies, industrial production index is likely to maintain its rising trend, helped by robust domestic consumption," Nomura economists say in a note. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry is scheduled to release the data at 2350 GMT.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 PREVIEW: Japan's industrial output likely fell 1.7% on-month in June, according to the median forecast of economists surveyed by Dow Jones and the Nikkei, as drops in auto production weigh on output. The fall comes after a revised 1.9% rise in May and marks the first drop since January. Still, the rising trend in industrial output is likely to continue, economists say. A corporate survey last month showed companies expect output to increase 3.3% on month in July. "Although we can't deny the possibility that production will come under pressure by a slowdown in overseas economies, industrial production index is likely to maintain its rising trend, helped by robust domestic consumption," Nomura economists say in a note. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry is scheduled to release the data at 2350 GMT.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals are mixed in tight ranges in Asia with trading remaining subdued and cautious ahead of China's official manufacturing data and Federal Open Market Committee meeting this week. "While we don't expect major changes to the FOMC policy statement, investors should expect volatility given the many factors in play," Kelly Teoh, a strategist at IG says in a report. Analysts note that though copper stockpiles have declined in July, soft economic outlook is likely to weigh on prices. Analysts say Chinese decision to shut down some copper production as the government restructures the economy, is also weighing on the economy. "We think copper prices could be at risk of a further sell-off this Thursday as China's official PMI data is announced, offsetting signs of good demand in China," ANZ says in a report. LME 3-month copper is at $6,836ton, down $24, while aluminum is at $1,798/ton, up $4 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is likely to hold its lending interest rate Tuesday, after tightening banking system liquidity earlier this month to protect the rupee. All 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expect the bank to keep its policy rate at 7.25% and cash reserve ratio at 4.0%. The RBI earlier this month set limits on the daily amount commercial banks can borrow from it, to make it more expensive for traders to buy U.S. dollars with borrowed rupees. Short-term interest rates and bond yields across maturities subsequently spiked. Further policy tightening will be "overkill, especially when the economy is already swooning from global headwinds and domestic weakening," says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 ; says further tightening could also derail government plans to narrow the fiscal deficit, as it could increase interest on government borrowing. Economists expect guidance on how long the liquidity measures will last, after the rate decision is announced at 0530 GMT Tuesday. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY is up after the Bank of Japan governor says easing is having a positive effect on the economy and inflation expectations, says a senior dealer at a Japanese bank. "There really isn't anything new in his comments, but markets were looking for cues to buy back the U.S. dollar after trending down so far today." USD/JPY is at 97.88 from an intraday low of 97.63; it may rise as far as 98.30 in the session, the dealer says. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  is likely to hold its lending interest rate Tuesday, after tightening banking system liquidity earlier this month to protect the rupee. All 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expect the bank to keep its policy rate at 7.25% and cash reserve ratio at 4.0%. The RBI earlier this month set limits on the daily amount commercial banks can borrow from it, to make it more expensive for traders to buy U.S. dollars with borrowed rupees. Short-term interest rates and bond yields across maturities subsequently spiked. Further policy tightening will be "overkill, especially when the economy is already swooning from global headwinds and domestic weakening," says \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 ; says further tightening could also derail government plans to narrow the fiscal deficit, as it could increase interest on government borrowing. Economists expect guidance on how long the liquidity measures will last, after the rate decision is announced at 0530 GMT Tuesday. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY is up after the Bank of Japan governor says easing is having a positive effect on the economy and inflation expectations, says a senior dealer at a Japanese bank. "There really isn't anything new in his comments, but markets were looking for cues to buy back the U.S. dollar after trending down so far today." USD/JPY is at 97.88 from an intraday low of 97.63; it may rise as far as 98.30 in the session, the dealer says. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Investors are awaiting several key data points this week, including a statement from Federal Reserve policy makers, U.S. jobs data and the official July reading on Chinese factory activity. Nomura says China's official purchasing managers index could be below 50 for the first time since September after a further weakening of China business sentiment this month. The bank pegs the July index at 49.2; a reading below 50 indicates contraction. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's "flash" July PMI report last week--which excludes state-owned enterprises and has a smaller sample size than the official report--printed at 47.7, a bigger-than-expected slump. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 29-07-13 0901GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTDJDU0020130729e97t000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
