\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Malaysia Shares End +0.2%; Eyes On Europe Woes - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2012\par 11:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares close up 0.2% at 1635.09, with buying from local funds in select blue chips plantation plays \uc2 Kuala Lumpur Kepong Bhd\uc2  (2445.KU) and \uc2 PPB Group Bhd\uc2  (4065.KU) driving the main index into positive territory, a local dealer says; "risk is still tilted toward the downside amid worries about Europe's sovereign debt," he says, tipping the main index to move in a 1620-1650 range in the near term. Market breadth is negative at 433 losers to 292 gainers with total volume of 881.5 million shares. \uc2 MMC Corp. Bhd\uc2  (2194.KU) ends down 2% at MYR2.47, \uc2 UEM Land Holdings Bhd\uc2  (5148.KU) slips 1% to MYR1.98 and Genting Malaysia Bhd (4715.KU) falls 0.9% to MYR3.43. Among gainers, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  ends up 1.9% at MYR24.16, PPB adds 1.8% to MYR15.48 and IHH Healthcare Holdings Bhd (5225.KU) gains 10% to MYR3.09, from its IPO price of MYR2.80. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end up 0.2% at 4000.839 in moderate volume, as local funds pick up some recently-battered stocks, while gains in telecommunication shares support the main index, traders say. Traders expects the market to consolidate Thursday in a 3970-4030 range. "A firmer rupiah and expectations of benign inflation for July (which will be announced next week) inspired local institutional funds to buy select blue chip stocks," says a fund manager at a local asset management company. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR664 billion worth of shares. Among gainers, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) is up 1.1% at IDR8,950 and gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) is up 1.3% at IDR3,825. The main index is up 4.7% year to date. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] SocGen says pay the short end of Hungary contracts, either via 3-month 6-month HUF forward-rate agreements or FRA, or via paying 2-year HUF interest rate swaps or IRS. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says that the market is getting carried away by pricing in some 35bp of monetary easing within three months and close to 100bp within a year vs the current 7% rate. SocGen does expects a rate cut once an IMF/EU package is in place, but says the hands of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be tied until then, being aware of the risks of the euro zone debt crisis.(veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] If the ESM were to receive a banking license, as speculation suggests, it would only be a minor palliative, for markets, rather than a lasting solution to the crisis in Nomura's view. It says ESM getting a license would be unlikely to dictate asset allocation strategies by investors. Any possible rally in lower-rated euro zone government bonds is likely to be limited and will provide an opportunity to investors to reduce their exposure, strategist Desmond Supple says. However, if the stop-gap measures are indeed pursued, we think it would at least hold the potential for extending the endgame for Spain beyond the current assumed 3-5 weeks," Supple says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan's economy likely grew at a slower pace in 2Q than the government had expected as external and domestic demand remained weak amid global economic slowdown and worries about the euro zone. Taiwan's GDP likely grew 0.40% on-year in 2Q, according to the median forecast of 14 economists in a Dow Jones poll, which would be slower than the government's previous estimate of 0.77%. Taiwan's GDP grew 0.39% in 1Q and 1.85% in 4Q 2011. Economists say weaker-than-expected exports and industrial output data in recent months suggest that the export-dependent economy hasn't recovered from weak demand for its products. "Sharp declines in imports of capital equipments indicated slowing domestic investment, while falling imports of consumer products also suggested weak private consumption...the second quarter and the full year economic growth will be much slower than the government's previous estimates," says Mega Securities economist Lucas Lee. The government is scheduled to issue preliminary 2Q GDP data Tuesday at 0030 GMT. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds are higher as investors continue to shift their money from equities to fixed-income assets amid growing concerns about Europe's debt problems; dealers say bulk of the buying activity remain focused in the mid- to long-end notes, flattening the yield curve further. "Malaysian bonds are considered to be the one of the safest investment within Southeast Asia... yields on seven- and 10-year bonds will probably fall further in the near term," says a dealer with a local bank. Investors will likely focus on the auction of August 2027 Government Securities worth MYR2.50 billion for trading cues; the tender will close on July 30 and the 15-year bond will be issued on July 31. The yield on October 2017 MGS is down 3 bps at 3.12% and September 2018 MGS yield is down 1 bp at 3.26%. August 2022 MGS yield is down 1 bp at 3.37% while October 2015 MGS yield is untraded with last yield at 3.02%. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT [Dow Jones] September gilts rally on the weak second-quarter UK GDP data, with the preliminary reading coming in at negative 0.7%--the biggest quarterly contraction since the Q1 2009--against estimates of negative 0.2%. The gilt contract jumped around 15 ticks higher on the release before fading back slightly to 122.01, up 10 ticks on the session. In the cash market, the yield on the interest-rate sensitive 2-year fell to a fresh low at 0.049%, while the 10-year benchmark traded at 1.45%, around 5bps off its all-time low yield.(nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] Placing Bund 2044 is "something of a challenge" says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  in the context of the current general low-yields environment. "The recent downgrade of Germany's sovereign rating outlook will also probably help make a convincing bid-to-cover ratio harder to achieve," adds \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  analyst Christian Reicherter ahead of Germany's EUR3 billion auction of the ultra-long Bund. One argument in favor of the issue is that the 10-30-year spread, currently around 90 basis points, has climbed to its highest level since September 2011, he adds. Auction results are due at 0930 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is higher at 9,500 vs 9,495 late Tuesday in Asia as investors continue to seek safety in the U.S. dollar amid persistent concerns over European debt problems. Dealers say Bank Indonesia was suspected of selling around $50 million in total Wednesday to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Bank Indonesia will likely defend the pair's 9,500 resistance," a dealer says. He tips the pair to stay in a 9,480-9,510 range Thursday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is higher at 3.1850 vs 3.1770 late Tuesday in Asia as investors adopt a risk-averse approach amid ongoing worries over Europe's debt problems. Still, the ringgit's downside could be limited as many players expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some stage to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a local trader says. He tips the pair to stay in a 3.1600-3.1900 range for the rest of the week. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD will fluctuate between 1.0125-10330 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  depending on market sentiment in the coming days. "In our view it is overstretched that markets are still pricing in rate cuts [in Australia] of approximately 100bps on a one year horizon. From this point of view the AUD still has upward scope," says the bank. But, "at present, external factors will provide direction for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it adds. AUD/USD trades at 1.0265. (alexandra.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD is nearing key support levels, notes \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analyst \uc2 Karen Jones\uc2 . Now at 1.5470, a major support line comes in at 1.5432, then it's the June 8 low of 1.5407, and the July low of 1.5393. Jones says a daily close below 1.5407 should be enough to trigger a fall to the 2012 low of 1.5235. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 25, 2012 05:35 ET (09:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120725e87p002nh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
