\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Malaysian Shares End Off 0.6%; 1590-1611 Band Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2012\par 11:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0936 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares close 0.6% lower at 1594.10 and are likely to move within a 1590-1611 range near-term as investors are still cautious ahead of an EU summit on Thursday and Friday, a local dealer says. Market breadth is negative at 472 losers to 274 gainers, with a total volume of 1.05 billion shares. Among losers, \uc2 YTL Corp. Bhd\uc2  (4677.KU) ends down 2.9% at MYR2.00, \uc2 PPB Group Bhd\uc2  (4065.KU) slides 1.9% to MYR15.74 and \uc2 Axiata Group Bhd\uc2  (6888.KU) falls 1.1% to MYR5.44. Among gainers, \uc2 AirAsia Bhd\uc2  (5099.KU) ends up 0.3% at MYR3.61, \uc2 RHB Capital Bhd\uc2  (1066.KU) rises 0.3% to MYR7.35 and \uc2 United Plantations Bhd\uc2  (2089.KU) adds 3.1% to MYR25.78. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end 0.6% higher at 3881.401 in light volume, led by bargain-buying from local institutional funds in most commodity-related and telecommunication stocks after recent falls, traders say. They expect the market to consolidate Wednesday in a 3850-3900 band. "Any positive developments from Europe may be a good catalyst for foreigners to return to the market and help lift the main index tomorrow," says a fund manager at a local asset management fund. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR216 billion worth of shares. Among gainers, nickel and gold miner Antam (ANTM.JK) is up 3.8% at IDR1,380 and coal miner Adaro (ADRO.JK) is 4.5% higher at IDR1,390 on bargain-buying. The main index is up 1.5% year-to-date. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] Repsol CDS and bonds look cheap, CreditSights says as it expects the company's debt instruments to outperform peers and other low BBB-rated names. CreditSights points out S&P's outlook revision on Repsol to stable from negative and its affirmation of the BBB- rating. "This view is in-line with the position we have had on Repsol since the company published its new strategic plan" in May, CreditSights says. Repsol's five-year CDS and cash bonds continue to trade at crossover levels, "which we currently view as cheap," CreditSights says. Repsol's five-year CDS are 11 bps wider at 510 bps according to Markit. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Spain's T-bill auction Tuesday underlines the country's ongoing difficulty in "financing itself at a sustainable level," notes Richard McGuire at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 . While 3- and 6-month bills were sold at yields significantly higher than at the May auctions, they were still "some way from the alarming levels of late last year." McGuire adds, "A failure to see much in the way of traction at this week's EU summit, as seems decidedly possible, will likely put further strong additional pressure on Spanish yields, thereby rapidly raising the prospect of additional bailouts over and above that requested to shore up the country's banking sector this week."(nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR falls to 9,450 vs 9,495 late Monday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say, adding that trade volume is thin ahead of the EU summit. Two dealers suspected Bank Indonesia to have sold below $50 million in the day to keep the pair from breaching the psychologically-important 9,500 mark. "The pair is likely to stay rangebound as investors await developments from the EU summit later this week," says a local dealer; "BI will try to curb the pair's rising to 9,500." He tips the pair in a 9,450-9,500 range Wednesday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] Measures announced by the RBI and the government Monday only increase the dependence on debt capital inflows and failed to meet expectations of tough reforms or policy decisions, says DBS. The house says while there are some positive developments on the external balance front such as the fall in crude prices and the correction in the INR, concerns remain. Falling prices may be accompanied by slowing demand for exports, while policy inertia of the government remains a concern, the house adds. Authorities Monday raised the investment limit for foreign institutions in government bonds to $20 billion from $15 billion and the aggregate limit for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to $40 billion from $30 billion. The spot pair is now 57.12 from 57.01 late Monday in Asia.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [Dow Jones] CZK trades little changed against EUR with EUR/CZK at 25.842-25.874, little changed from 25.865 late Monday and investors are seen keeping to sidelines ahead of the Thursday's rate decision by the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Most analysts expect the bank to lower its base rate 25 bps to a new record low of 0.50%. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is slightly lower at 3.1942, off an intraday high of 3.2000, and vs 3.1950 late Monday in Asia as \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the fall in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  say two dealers; they suspect Bank Negara to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 3.1960. The pair is tipped to remain rangebound as investors await developments from the EU summit later this week; any upside will likely be limited due to selling interest by exporters for trade settlements, a trader at a local bank says, and tips the pair in a 3.1910-3.2000 band near-term. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is down late Tuesday, weighed by the EUR and some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains versus the USD. The pair is at 42.47 after settling late Monday at 42.64. "But we should see the dollar rebound in the coming days due to month-end dollar requirements and ahead of the EU summit," says a local bank trader. Volume is still heavy but lower compared to Monday's. Traders expect the USD/PHP to move within a 42.30-42.70 band in the coming days, with a stronger USD likely. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys follow Bunds lower in London trade, as safe-haven bonds pare Monday's gains which were made as hopes of meaningful progress at this week's EU summit faded. Tuesday's two-year auction should go well, as the recent weak performance of the sector has left it looking cheap, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says. US consumer confidence data due at 1400 GMT. September Treasurys are down 6/32 at 133-120, and the 10-year cash yield is 1.62%. (tommy.stubbington@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  reiterates its expectation of more Israeli cuts after the Bank of Israel announced a 25bp rate cut Monday. Benoit Anne, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , sees the accompanying dovish statement as a promise of more. SocGen expects another 25bp cut, probably as soon as August. USD/ILS trades at 3.931. (laura.clarke@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD trims some of its earlier losses late in the session, after Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying dollars around 29.950, lifting the pair to 29.990 from its intraday low of 29.930, two local traders say; the pair was at 29.995 late Monday in Asia. One of the traders says exporters are keen to sell the greenback from 29.950, taking some steam out of the pair's recovery; she says exporters' month-end settlement will continue to cap the pair at 30.000 probably for this week, "unless there is a sudden turn for the worse in Europe." The trader tips the USD/TWD to remain in a 29.90-30.00 band Wednesday. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 26, 2012 05:36 ET (09:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120626e86q00295\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
