\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Malaysia 3Q GDP +5.8% On Year; Economists Expected +4.7%\b0\par\par\uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2011\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov Zeti: Inflation Seems To Have Peaked\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Inflation Likely To Remain "Relatively Stable"; Moderate In 2012\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Interest Rates Remain Supportive Of Growth\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Domestic Demand To Anchor Growth Going Forward\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Risk To Global Growth Has Increased\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: 4Q Growth Prospects Uncertain, But Domestic Demand Remains Strong\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Volatility In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Likely To Persist For A Long Period\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Fundamental Issues In Developed Economies To Cause \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Volatility\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Zeti: Will \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market To Smooth Out Excessive Volatility\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Zeti: Won't Influence Underlying Trend Of Exchange Rate\par \par \uc2 18 Nov 2011 05:00 EDT DJ  DATA SNAP: Malaysia 3Q GDP 5.8% On Year; Economists Expected 4.7%\par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Malaysia's economy expanded 5.8% year-on-year in the third quarter, much faster than market expectations and accelerating from the revised second-quarter growth rate, data released Friday show.\par \par \uc2 The 14 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had expected growth of 4.7% for the July-September period from a year earlier. During the second quarter, Malaysia's gross domestic product grew at a revised 4.3% on year.\par \par \uc2 The agriculture sector was the main contributor to on-year growth in the third quarter, the Department of Statistics said in a statement, while the mining sector was a drag.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia Gov. Zeti Akhtar Aziz, speaking at a press conference announcing the release of the data, said, "The risks to global economic growth increased following developments in the third quarter and it is likely that some of the impact of this will be experienced in the fourth quarter."\par \par \uc2 She also said, "Indicators show that consumption demand is likely to continue, private and public investments have gathered momentum in the third quarter and we expect it to continue in the fourth quarter."\par \par \uc2 She also said the impact of slowing external demand on Malaysia's economic growth remains to be seen. "Malaysia is a highly open economy and it will affect our external sector," she said.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, Malaysia's economy, the third-largest in Southeast Asia, grew 7.2%, reversing from the previous year's 1.7% contraction. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Following are selected economic indicators released by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par                                  Year-on-year change \par                                Third Qtr     Second Qtr \par Gross Domestic Product           +5.8%         +4.3% \par Manufacturing                    +5.1%         +2.1% \par Services sector                  +7.0%         +6.8% \par Mining sector                    -6.1%         -9.2% \par Agriculture sector               +8.2%         +6.9% \par  \par Private Sector Consumption       +7.3%         +6.4% \par Public Sector Consumption        +21.7%        +6.6% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By 