\par\par \b \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS-Malaysia c.bank governor Q3 GDP press conference\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2011\par 11:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR, Nov 18 (Reuters) - Following are the highlights from a press conference by Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zeti Akthar Aziz:\par \par \uc2 * On the Q 3 performance:\par \par \uc2 "Despite the (external) challenges, Malaysia registered third quarter growth of 5.8 percent supported by mainly stronger domestic demand, higher private sector expenditure and higher public spending."\par \par \uc2 * On prosepects for fourth quarter:\par \par \uc2 "The risk to global economic growth have increased and following developments that have taken place in the third quarter it is very likely that some of the impact of this will be experienced in the fourth quarter and this will indeed happen as Malaysia is a highly open economy."\par \par \uc2 "However we expect that our domestic economy and domestic demand will continue to sustain its growth. Indicators show that consumption demand is likely to continue and private sector investment and also public investment has gathered momentum. We saw in the first half of the year this was slow. This has gathered momentum in the third quarter and we will continue to see it in the fourth quarter."\par \par \uc2 * On whether Malaysia will see 6 pct GDP growth this year which it needs to in order to achieve developed nation status in 2020 and the country's economic transformation programme goal:\par \par \uc2 "This has already been revised in the budget to 5.0-5.5 percent and given the very recent developments in Europe, even if we have a rate of growth of 5 percent or even the high 4, 4.8 or 4.9, this will be considered very good growth in such a challenging environment."\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Is Bank\uc2  Negara Malaysia considering re-establishing US dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines with the U.S. Federal reserve in light of the current situation:\par \par \uc2 "At this stage we don't have any plans, there's adequate dollar liquidity, and we have other arrangements within the region, other swap arrangements and we believe that for us that is sufficient."\par \par \uc2 * On outlook for growth in 2012:\par \par \uc2 "The external environment is challenging and uncertain and therefore this is why we need to make a careful assessment and monitor very closely to what extent the global developments will affect our overall economic growth and to what extent the strength of the domestic economy can offset the impact of these developments on our trade sector."\par \par \uc2 * On whether BNM will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event of sharp volatility in the ringgit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 "Most central banks in our region have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do so in the event of excessive movements in any one day that will be destabilising to our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. But our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is entirely to smooth out these excessive volatitlity or movement in any one particular day and we do not influence the underlying trend of the excange rate."\par \par \uc2 * Elaborating on the external risk and how exactly it will affect the economy:\par \par \uc2 "The disruptions and destabilising effects are through many channels. Of course, it's through the trade channel but Europe mainly trades with itself among countries in the Eurozone rather than with other parts of the world, and therefore that is not going to be having such a significant impact. But because of the uncertainties generated by the volatility in the financial markets this is destabilising."\par \par \uc2 * On key interest rate outlook:\par \par \uc2 "At this point, out interest rate at 3.00 percent remains supportive of growth and unless there are any other significant developments that pose a major risk to our domestic growth and if there are any developments in inflation this will determine interest rate policy, but for now the current interest rate remains supportive of growth and that is important for our economy."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Razak Ahmad; Editing by Niluksi Koswanage)\par \par \uc2 MALAYSIA-ECONOMY/HIGHLIGHTS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111118e7bi000me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
