\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit, baht breach L-T support; Asia outlook weak\b0\par\par\uc2 1,275 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2011\par 10:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Ringgit, baht break through 100-week moving averages\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Taiwan, Philippine spotted\par \par \uc2 * Hedge funds, real money, leveraged hit Sing dlr\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds snippets)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit and the Thai baht breached long-term support lines on Monday, while the Taiwan dollar and the Singapore dollar hit 10-month lows, as offshore funds cut risk exposures further in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid increased worries about the European crisis.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities including central banks of Malaysia and Taiwan were spotted selling dollars to limit falls in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Still, players are abandoning riskier assets and looking for opportunities to sell emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as concerns over the festering debt crisis in Europe and the slackening global economy roils markets.\par \par \uc2 Unnerved investors are opting for safe haven USD-denominated assets by dumping stocks, commodities and other growth-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar .\par \par \uc2 "I will buy USD on dips unless we see really concrete measures coming out from Europe," said Kenneth Kan, head of emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Credit Agricole Corporate and Investment Bank\uc2  in Singapore, adding real money funds still hold short dollar/Asia exposure against their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed income and equity holdings.\par \par \uc2 On regional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kan said, "They won't be too keen to deplete their USD reserves too quickly, so USD selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general is not as easy compared to USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Asian central banks."\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been hit by strong dollar-demand in non-deliverable forwards (NDFs) from offshore institutional investors, which in the past weeks have rushed to hedge against further falls in the regional units. Some funds built up dollar-long positions against them.\par \par \uc2 In the third quarter, the Taiwan dollar suffered its largest quarterly fall against the U.S. dollar since the Asian financial crisis in 1997, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 The South Korean won, too, suffered its worst quarterly loss since the third quarter of the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Asian components were not the only victims. Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also suffered as Brazil's real raked up the world's worst performance in September.\par \par \uc2 The weakness in the emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued even after traders spotted dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the regional central banks.\par \par \uc2 Dealers and analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be as strong as before as growth slows both inside and outside. Earlier this year, Asian central banks and governments had been seen selling dollars to contain inflation.\par \par \uc2 "I believe Asian central banks are all very happy (with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls) as they will worry if their export numbers drop at some point with the euro zone's slowdown," said a senior dealer at an Asian bank in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 Adding to worries about a slackening growth, September's factory activity in some of Asia's biggest economies slumped to levels last seen during the depths of the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's inflation slowed in September but the authorities are under pressure to stabilise financial markets as outflows have increased in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 After continuous falls in emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some dealers see possibilities of rebounds with many of them staying in technically oversold territory, albiet only for a very short term.\par \par \uc2 "The rise (in Dollar/Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is getting a bit tired. In the short term, I will probably play from the short side with tight stops," said a European bank dealer in Singapore, adding he plans to take profit "very quickly".\par \par \uc2 "But this strategy is purely short term. Should conditions in Europe continue to deteriorate, Asia will continue to see outflows from real money."\par \par \uc2 Financial markets in China and South Korea are closed for holidays.\par \par \uc2 RINGGIT, BAHT\par \par \uc2 The ringgit and the baht broke through 100-week moving averages, indicating further falls.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened past 3.1560 per dollar and has room to soften more, probably to 3.2400, around the 38.2 percent Fibonacci retracement of its appreciation between March 2009 and August this year.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars at 3.2070, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Dollar-long positions against the ringgit were second-largest after dollar/rupee, a Reuters' poll showed last week.\par \par \uc2 The baht also weakened the average of 31.26 versus the greenback on selling by foreign banks and real money funds. It has crossed this technical level for the first time since September 2009.\par \par \uc2 The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken to 32.10, the 38.2 percent retracement of its strengthening between March 2009 and November last year. It has minor support around 31.45 and 31.90.\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar slid as hedge funds, real money accounts, leveraged names and interbank speculators offloaded.\par \par \uc2 The city-states' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to as soft as 1.3139, the weakest since December last year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not spotted selling the U.S. dollars, dealers said, but they remained wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially around 1.3200 per the greenback.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has a 100-week moving average at 1.3188. The 14-day U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar relative strength index (RSI) rose to 74.9, indicating the Singapore dollar is in oversold territory.\par \par \uc2 "Given the risk off environment, most players will continue to unwind shorts and some are adding long USD (positions) as well. But I think 1.3150-1.3200 topside may be limited," said a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar hit a ten-month low to weaken to near a 100-week moving average on stock outflows.\par \par \uc2 The island's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to as soft as 30.672 per the U.S. dollar, the weakest since Dec last year.\par \par \uc2 But the local unit recovered some of earlier losses as it is seen finding support around 30.670 on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exporters.\par \par \uc2 Some foreign bank took profit from the recent gains in the U.S. dollar around the level, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar also has a 100-week moving average at 30.727.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan stocks lost nearly three percent with foreign investors selling a net T$13.1 billion ($429.9 million).\par \par \uc2 Last month, foreign investors were net sellers with total selling at T$78.68 billion. That was the fifth straight month of net selling.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINE PESO\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso fell against the dollar on risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited, dealers said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0740 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.88          77.03       +0.20\par  Sing dlr        1.3100         1.3078       -0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      30.578         30.506       -0.24\par *Korean won     1180.90        1178.10       -0.24\par  Baht             31.28          31.06       -0.70\par  Peso             43.97          43.72       -0.57\par  Rupiah         8925.00        8780.00       -1.62\par  Rupee            49.36          48.93       -0.87\par  Ringgit         3.2050         3.1890       -0.50\par *Yuan            6.3780         6.3859       +0.12\par  Change so far in 2011\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        76.88          81.15       +5.55\par  Sing dlr        1.3100         1.2820       -2.14\par  Taiwan dlr      30.578         30.368       -0.69\par  Korean won     1180.90        1134.80       -3.90\par  Baht             31.28          30.14       -3.64\par  Peso             43.97          43.84       -0.30\par  Rupiah         8925.00        9005.00       +0.90\par  Rupee            4