\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Malaysia 1Q GDP Up 4.6% On Year; Economists Expected +4.8%\b0\par\par\uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2011\par 12:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia 1Q Manufacturing Output Up 5.4%; Services Up 5.9%\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia Keeps 2011 GDP Growth Forecast At 5%-6%\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov: Keeping 2011 Average Inflation Forecast At 2.5%-3.5%\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov: Inflation May Be Near The Top End Of Guidance\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov: Not Relying On \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movements To Control Inflation\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov: \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Markets To Ensure Orderly Movements\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT *DJ  Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov: Current Interest Rates Supportive Of Growth\par \par \uc2 18 May 2011 06:00 EDT DJ  DATA SNAP: Malaysia 1Q GDP Up 4.6% On Yr; +4.8% Expected\par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR (Dow Jones)--Malaysia's economy expanded 4.6% from a year earlier in the first quarter, a slower-than-expected pace that was mainly due to a mild slowdown in the manufacturing sector, data released Wednesday show.\par \par \uc2 The 12 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had given a median forecast of 4.8% expansion in the first quarter, matching the previous quarter's growth.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, Malaysia's economy grew 7.2%, beating an official forecast of 7.0% in a reversal from the previous year's 1.7% contraction. The government forecasts the trade-driven economy to grow between 5.0% and 6.0% this year.\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia Gov. Zeti Akhtar Aziz, speaking at a press conference announcing the release of the data, said "In the first quarter global economic growth has continued but at a slower pace. While growth in the advanced economies has been modest, in the emerging economies economic activity has continued to be robust supported by domestic demand and augmented by stronger than expected export performance."\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's first-quarter GDP growth was "driven by strong domestic demand and improved external sector performance. Domestic demand was supported by sustained private sector spending. The external demand during the quarter was supported by sustained strong regional demand for commodities and non E&E products," Zeti added, referring to electricals and electronic goods.\par \par \uc2 "There were however some emerging risks to the global recovery which resulted in increased volatility in the global financial market during the quarter. These include the alleviated commodity prices that has resulted in higher inflation, sovereign debt conditions in several advanced economies, the disaster in Japan and its impact on the production chain across the world and the surges in capital flows to emerging economies," she said.\par \par \uc2 She, however, voiced confidence that Malaysia's "strong economic fundamentals and policy flexibility" will enable the domestic economy to perform steadily this year.\par \par \uc2 For the first quarter, she said the consumer price index rose 2.8% on an annualized basis.\par \par \uc2 Following are selected economic indicators released by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                First Qtr      Fourth Qtr \par Gross Domestic Product           +4.6%         +4.8% \par Manufacturing                    +5.4%         +6.2% \par Services sector                  +5.9%         +6.1% \par Mining sector                    -3.0%         -1.2% \par Agriculture sector               -0.3%         -3.9% \par Construction sector              +3.8%         +5.6% \par  \par Private Sector Consumption       +6.7%         +6.4% \par Public Sector Consumption        +6.1%         +0.1% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ankur Relia; Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2026-1233; ankur.relia@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 18, 2011 06:00 ET (10:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110518e75i00274\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
