\par\par \b \uc2 DJ US Dollar To Remain Pressured Despite Singapore  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Move\b0\par\par\uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2011\par 05:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Asian central banks unlikely to end dollar recycling\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move not likely to herald regional shift in policy\par \par \uc2 -- U.S. dollar set to remain under pressure By Siva Sithraputhran Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar is set to remain under pressure over the long term because most Asian central banks are seen as being unlikely to slow the pace of greenback sales, even after Singapore signaled that it might do just that.\par \par \uc2 Asian central banks typically buy the dollar against local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow any export-denting bouts of strength, but then churn the dollars into euros, Australian dollars, and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spread the risks, so the greenback falls. This 'recycling' of reserves has been at the heart of the dollar's weakness against a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --including the euro--over recent years.\par \par \uc2 That relationship came under scrutiny Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore opened up to more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength by raising the trading band of the Singapore dollar, initially boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a record high against the greenback and dragging other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher with it. Tellingly though, the immediate reaction from Singapore's closest neighbor, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stem the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the ringgit--indicating that there is still lot of resistance to allow local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.\par \par \uc2 Recycling quickly kicked in, and the U.S. dollar plunged, sinking fast against the euro, the Australian dollar, and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Valentin Marinov, a strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in London, remains of the opinion that emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be allowed to rise only gradually, maintaining that pressure on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 'This set-up could be particularly damaging for the dollar against its liquid alternatives in the G-10 world,' he said. 'We think this should continue to generate sufficient upside momentum for the euro and Australian dollar against the greenback,' he added.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few months, there have been waves of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of South Korea, Singapore, Thailand, Indonesia and Malaysia to arrest the rises of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising too fast, the central banks want to make sure their exports don't lose competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 'There is no monetary coordination in Asia, with most policymakers running mercantilist-type policies that favor their own exports via undervalued exchange rates,' Win Thin, New York-based strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Singapore has been 'more open to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in [a] controlled way' than some regional competitors, said Ken Dickson, investment director at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2  in Edinburgh, which manages GBP143 billion in assets worldwide.\par \par \uc2 To some extent, China too has been allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise very gradually as it tries to rein in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves held by Asian central banks has continued to mount, suggesting that the wider market impact of any region-wide change in policy could be huge, even if only a fraction of these reserves are moved in or out of dollars.\par \par \uc2 At the end of March, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged to a record, topping $3 trillion. In Singapore, reserves have been climbing steadily since November, to reach just over $233 billion in March. That indicates that the central banks have been carrying out smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the required trading bands. Data for April will be eagerly awaited for signs that the pace of gains has moderated.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's move last week means it will be able to trim these smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Dickson at Standard Life.\par \par \uc2 But as other regional central banks are still loathe to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, the impact of Singapore's action on the U.S. dollar looks set to be drowned out. -By Siva Sithraputhran, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-20-7842-9462; siva.sithraputhran@dowjones.com.\par \par \uc2 (Rose Yu in Shanghai contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110421e74k003h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
