\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Singapore Tightens Monetary Policy To Fight Inflation As GDP Growth Surges\b0\par\par\uc2 1,166 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2011\par 06:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightens monetary policy\par \par \uc2 -- MAS raises Singapore dollar trading band, leaves slope and width of the band unchanged\par \par \uc2 -- Government reports GDP growth surged to an annualized pace of 23.5% on quarter from 3.9% in the previous quarter\par \par \uc2 -- Singapore dollar jumps to fresh record high vs U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  ringgit surges, inviting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds economist comments in paragraphs four and five, Malaysia ringgit reaction in paragraph 11.)\par \par \uc2 By P.R. Venkat and Gaurav Raghuvanshi\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore tightened its monetary policy Thursday as it posted a sharp acceleration in economic growth for the first quarter, intensifying its fight against inflationary pressures that have continued to build on high global commodities prices.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to strengthen the Singapore dollar, its main monetary policy lever, sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a record high against the U.S. dollar, and marked the latest effort by authorities in Asia to try to curb rising consumer prices that have clouded the region's economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's move wasn't as aggressive as its two decisions last year to tighten policy, which set the Singapore dollar on an upward course. But it reflected concern about a surge in inflation, which hit 5.2% in the first two months of the year, and the upside risks from high oil prices and reduced slack in the booming domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 "It would have been hard for the MAS to ignore the extraordinarily strong first quarter growth number," said Robert Prior-Wandesforde, an economist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "After the latest tightening move, we believe that the MAS will become gradually more relaxed about inflation, which we believe is likely to have peaked. With rising oil prices likely to have an impact (on growth) and the Japan crisis perhaps a bigger issue than most people expect, we doubt the MAS will tighten further in October," he said. But he added: "it's still early days" to think about the October review.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's economy, a bellwether for the regional economy, grew at an annualized pace of 23.5% in January-March from the previous quarter, accelerating from a 3.9% expansion in the last quarter of 2010 after adjustment for seasonal factors, the Ministry of Trade and Industry said.\par \par \uc2 The expansion in gross domestic product was much faster than expected by market participants, beating all 11 analyst estimates in a Dow Jones Newswires poll. The median forecast in the poll was for a 12.4% expansion.\par \par \uc2 The MAS raised its trading band for the Singapore dollar and left the width and the slope of the band unchanged. It recentered the band below the level of the Singapore dollar's nominal effective exchange rate that prevailed before the announcement, a move it said that took into account its policy moves last year.\par \par \uc2 "This policy will ensure price stability in the medium term while keeping growth on a sustainable path," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose sharply on the news, surging to a new record against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as low as S$1.2453 immediately after the announcement, down from S$1.2535 earlier in the morning. At 0420 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.2491.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit, which closely tracks the Singapore dollar, surged after the MAS decision, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar, traders said. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as low as MYR3.0180 moments after the MAS announcement, though it later recovered to around MYR3.0220.\par \par \uc2 The MAS has been targeting a "modest and gradual appreciation" of the Singapore dollar against its undisclosed traded-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April last year. Analysts estimate Thursday's move revalued the Singapore dollar by about 1.5% and keeps it on an upward trajectory.\par \par \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  expects the U.S. dollar to fall to S$1.23 in about a month. DBS forecasts the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will reach that level by the end of June. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  expects it may take the rest of the year for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall that far but said there is risk the slide may be faster.\par \par \uc2 The MAS guides the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band, using the exchange rate rather than interest rates as its monetary-policy tool because foreign trade dwarfs domestic demand in island's nearly US$225 billion economy.\par \par \uc2 Eleven of 14 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires ahead of the MAS announcement said they expected some form of tightening involving a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Eight of those predicted the MAS would recenter the midpoint of the trading band. Three expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold policy steady.\par \par \uc2 The move by Singapore comes amid an effort by global central banks to head off inflation, which has been fueled by higher prices of oil and commodities. In Asia, most central banks are well into tightening cycles, with many widely expected to hike interest rates further in coming months. Earlier this month, China raised its interest rates for a fourth time in six months. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Thursday hiked rates for the first time since 2008.\par \par \uc2 The MAS said economic activity in Singapore will likely remain high for the rest of the year, though underlying growth momentum will moderate. It warned that cost and price pressures will remain firm and predicted global oil and food prices will remain high.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it expects CPI inflation be at the upper half of its 3%-4% forecast this year, while core inflation will be 2%-3%. Headline inflation will likely remain elevated and moderate only gradually to around 3% by fourth quarter of the year, it said.\par \par \uc2 The MAS, which sets its monetary policy twice a year, tightened at both meetings last year to counter a pickup in inflation as the economy staged a sharp recovery from the global financial crisis. Last April, the MAS re-centered its policy band at a higher level and shifted to its stance of targeting a "modest and gradual appreciation" of the local dollar from zero appreciation. In October, it retained the same language but increased the slope and width of its exchange rate policy band.\par \par \uc2 DBS Bank said robust sequential growth in GDP suggests that underlying momentum has remained fairly robust despite some near term growth concerns.\par \par \uc2 The data "further reinforced our long held view that overall GDP growth for this year will remain strong and will most likely exceed the official forecast range of 4-6%," DBS Bank said, adding it expects Singapore's economy to expand 7.0% this year.\par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat and Gaurav Raghuvanshi; Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 154; gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com [ 14-04-11 0432GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110414e74e0007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
