\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Bonds End Up; 128 As Index Cap Next Week\b0\par\par\uc2 1,249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2011\par 10:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up amid easing concerns over further tightening measures by Beijing near-term, says a Beijing-based analyst with a local fund; "the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't use interest rate increases too frequently, so a further interest rate hike is unlikely in the weeks ahead (following a 25 bp hike earlier this week)." The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 127.71, up from Thursday close of 127.69. It is likely to meet resistance at 128.00 next week as inflationary pressures persist. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR falls further and is now at 3.0210 from its morning high of 3.0310 and late Thursday's 3.0280, tracking gains in the Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the USD. Two traders say \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  is suspected of buying dollars at 3.0210 and 3.0220 levels to slow the rise in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping a tight watch. We think the 3.0200 level will act as a strong support," one trader says. He tips a 3.0200-3.0300 range for next week, and says investors will be watching the SGD's moves as MYR has been tracking the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last couple of weeks.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds are a tad higher late on mild bargain-hunting, says a dealer with a local bank; "the auction size of MYR3 billion for the April 2026 MGS was lower than the MYR4 billion the market had expected, and that sparked some buying." He adds, focus is on shorter-term paper such as Bank Negara notes, due to a firmer MYR (USD/MYR at 3.0210 vs 3.0280 late Thursday), from portfolio fund inflows. The dealer expects the ringgit bonds to hold steady Monday ahead of the February industrial output data due 0401 GMT. The yield on August 2014 MGS is flat at 3.36%, the yield on the September 2016 MGS is down 1 bp at 3.61% and the yield on the September 2017 MGS is down 2 bps at 3.80%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones]--The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep inflation within 7.5% this year if the government does not increase budget expenditures, Troika Dialog says. Disinflation will be helped by the regulator's more flexible ruble policy--the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $10.7 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter, compared with $60 billion in the second quarter of 2008, the last time oil prices exceeded $100 per barrel, Troika points out. Consumer prices up 3.9% so far this year. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF faces key resistance at 1.3140, but should this level be breached, has upside potential to 1.3180, according to \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "Above 1.3180 we could see buying emerging, taking the pair up to the key level of 1.3240," says technical analyst Christian Eugster. Downside support is seen at 1.3065, and then 1.2960, he adds. EUR/CHF at 1.3149. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are down, taking cues from bunds as higher equities damp demand for assets perceived to be safer. UK producer price data at 0830 GMT is the only release of note Friday. June gilts are down 0.30 at 116.33 and Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets sees support for the contract at 116.05 and 115.84. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY bullish momentum is running at a 15-year high and that would explain why the cross is back up at Wednesday's 122.62 highs says \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 's Nicole Elliott. The cross is now extremely overbought, however, a weekly close above 122.00 should trigger another strong rally toward a measured target of 126.00 says Elliott. For a strategy she favors buying at market, adding at 122.00 with a stop below 120.70, adding to longs on a break of 122.65 to target 126.00. EUR/JPY now at 122.75. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] The increase in euro zone interest rates should give the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  more room to tighten its own monetary policy in June without pushing the franc higher, says \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "The SNB has cited the rise in property prices as a key argument for raising Swiss interest rates," notes ZKB. The ECB Thursday lifted its refinancing rate by a quarter of a percentage point to 1.25%. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower at 7.7679 vs 7.7736 in late Asian trade Thursday, tracking the greenback's weakness vs the EUR following the ECB's rate hike Thursday, while strong stocks continue to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a trader at a local bank. The HSI is up 0.6% at 24,414.64, after setting a fresh year-to-date high of 24,468.64 earlier in the day. "Upcoming IPOs in HK are also supporting the Hong Kong dollar," he says. The trader expects the pair to trade in a 7.7650-7.7720 range in the near term. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower in the afternoon, as USD sales continue to dominate amid a waning dollar index. The pair is at 6.5370 from 6.5420 late Thursday. "The string of lower fixings since end-March indicates that the PBOC is allowing the yuan to appreciate much quicker," says a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank. He adds that the USD/CNY offshore NDF is picking up on that trend, by trading at a three-year low of 6.3600/6.3700, down from 6.3990/6.4020 late Thursday. The PBOC fixing is set at 6.5420 vs 6.5456 Thursday. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is stronger and will likely continue to benefit from expectations of further monetary tightening in Poland as early as in May, says BRE. The Polish MPC has turned rather aggressive with its April increase of 25 basis points, reducing market expectations that it will be behind the ECB with tightening, BRE adds. EUR/USD is higher, which also helps the zloty. EUR/PLN is at 3.9491 from 3.9658 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 2.7426 from 2.7735. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] The combination of Thursday's ECB rate hike and the budget deadlock in Washington proved too much for the USD overnight, which sank wholesale. EUR/USD and GBP/USD made fresh one year highs, AUD/USD breached 1.05 for the first time in 28 years, and USD/CAD sank to a fresh 3 1/2 year low. It was no better for the greenback in Asia, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Philippines, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korea and Thailand to try and stop the rot. For Friday, UK March PPI is the data interest, while the informal Ecofin meeting in Budapest is likely to throw out some interesting headlines. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-11 0416ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110408e7480005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
