.3180 we could see buying emerging, taking the pair up to the key level of 1.3240," says technical analyst Christian Eugster. Downside support is seen at 1.3065, and then 1.2960, he adds. EUR/CHF at 1.3149. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are down, taking cues from bunds as higher equities damp demand for assets perceived to be safer. UK producer price data at 0830 GMT is the only release of note Friday. June gilts are down 0.30 at 116.33 and Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets sees support for the contract at 116.05 and 115.84. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY bullish momentum is running at a 15-year high and that would explain why the cross is back up at Wednesday's 122.62 highs says \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 's Nicole Elliott. The cross is now extremely overbought, however, a weekly close above 122.00 should trigger another strong rally toward a measured target of 126.00 says Elliott. For a strategy she favors buying at market, adding at 122.00 with a stop below 120.70, adding to longs on a break of 122.65 to target 126.00. EUR/JPY now at 122.75. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] The increase in euro zone interest rates should give the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  more room to tighten its own monetary policy in June without pushing the franc higher, says \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "The SNB has cited the rise in property prices as a key argument for raising Swiss interest rates," notes ZKB. The ECB Thursday lifted its refinancing rate by a quarter of a percentage point to 1.25%. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower at 7.7679 vs 7.7736 in late Asian trade Thursday, tracking the greenback's weakness vs the EUR following the ECB's rate hike Thursday, while strong stocks continue to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a trader at a local bank. The HSI is up 0.6% at 24,414.64, after setting a fresh year-to-date high of 24,468.64 earlier in the day. "Upcoming IPOs in HK are also supporting the Hong Kong dollar," he says. The trader expects the pair to trade in a 7.7650-7.7720 range in the near term. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower in the afternoon, as USD sales continue to dominate amid a waning dollar index. The pair is at 6.5370 from 6.5420 late Thursday. "The string of lower fixings since end-March indicates that the PBOC is allowing the yuan to appreciate much quicker," says a Shanghai-based trader at a foreign bank. He adds that the USD/CNY offshore NDF is picking up on that trend, by trading at a three-year low of 6.3600/6.3700, down from 6.3990/6.4020 late Thursday. The PBOC fixing is set at 6.5420 vs 6.5456 Thursday. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is stronger and will likely continue to benefit from expectations of further monetary tightening in Poland as early as in May, says BRE. The Polish MPC has turned rather aggressive with its April increase of 25 basis points, reducing market expectations that it will be behind the ECB with tightening, BRE adds. EUR/USD is higher, which also helps the zloty. EUR/PLN is at 3.9491 from 3.9658 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 2.7426 from 2.7735. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] The combination of Thursday's ECB rate hike and the budget deadlock in Washington proved too much for the USD overnight, which sank wholesale. EUR/USD and GBP/USD made fresh one year highs, AUD/USD breached 1.05 for the first time in 28 years, and USD/CAD sank to a fresh 3 1/2 year low. It was no better for the greenback in Asia, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Philippines, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Korea and Thailand to try and stop the rot. For Friday, UK March PPI is the data interest, while the informal Ecofin meeting in Budapest is likely to throw out some interesting headlines. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore STI sustains gains and the index is up 0.2% at 3178.12 in the post-lunch session. Traders expect the benchmark index to stay in positive territory for the rest of the session. "The sentiment is still positive and the Asian markets are flush with liquidity. The global economy is slowly reverting back to normal, which should continue to boost Singapore shares in the medium term," says a trader with a local brokerage. However, he flagged the risk of high oil prices hurting economic sentiment in the export dependant nation. Resistance is tipped at 3200 and support is below 3165. Among top gainers, SGX (S68.SG) is up 2.5% at S$8.35% after Australia formally blocked merger plans with ASX (ASX.AU). \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG) is up 2.8% at S$5.50 and its rig building unit \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) is up 1.0% at S$5.99 on strong crude prices. \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG) is up 0.2% at S$12.70. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] An "easy solution" to the problem of negotiating a Portuguese bailout package ahead of the country's June 5 election would be for the country to get a bridge loan "of a few billions" from the EU, to take it through to July, says Alessandro Giansanti at ING. Without a stable majority government, it "will be not easy for Portugal to form a consensus around a structural reform agenda, plus a commitment to fiscal consolidation." Portugal 2-year bonds yield 8.89%. (mark.brown@dowjones.com) [ 04-08-11 0407ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110408e74800056\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
