sistance. Market breadth is positive with 489 gainers vs 310 losers. Among gainers Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) rose 2.4% at MYR3.77, \uc2 Genting Bhd\uc2  (3182.KU) was up 1.8% at MYR11.22 and \uc2 MAA Holdings\uc2  (1198.KU) added 22.4% to MYR1.20 on a New Straits Times report it may sell its insurance unit to Zurich Insurance. Among losers, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) lost 3.0% to MYR5.55 and Gamuda (5398.KU) was off 0.8% at MYR3.87. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys are little changed in light European trading, a trader says. "Volumes are low and the range is tight. There is plenty for the market to think about, but its coming further down the line." Fed hawk Bullard's comments that he will push for a $100 billion cut in the quantitative easing program are outweighed by Bernanke's commitment on seeing the program through. "The FOMC minutes at the end of the month will be crucial, but there's nothing like that to focus on near-term, except the ECB," the trader adds. 10-year Treasury yields are flat at 3.48% and the June contract is up 0.05 at 118-250. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP hit a 5-month low late Wednesday, weighed by equity-related inflows, remittances from overseas Filipino workers, and also as the EUR advances against the USD. The pair is at 43.13 from 43.35 late Tuesday in heavy volume. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprisingly allowed the dollar/peso to breach the 43.25 major support, so we're expecting further downside tomorrow," says a local bank trader. He tips next support at 42.90 with resistance at 43.25. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts rise after UK manufacturing data come in weaker than expected. Manufacturing output in February was flat on the month vs expectations for a 0.7% rise. June gilts are up 0.16 at 116.74 vs 116.59 before the data. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is down, but off the nearly six-month low touched earlier in the session, on custodial flows, says a senior dealer with a foreign bank. The pair is at 44.23 after falling as low as 44.175, last touched on Oct. 18, compared with 44.43 late Tuesday. "Apart from the flows, the euro and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the Korean won, have strengthened, which is helping the INR. Though 44.18-44.20 levels are presenting a good buying opportunity for oil-importing companies," he says. He expects the pair in a broad range of 44.15-44.30 for the rest of the session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] Before its Swiss CPI-fueled collapse early Wednesday, the future looked bright for EUR/CHF. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says with the cross breaching its 200-day MA at 1.3145 and also overcoming the 1.3205 February high, a close below here Wednesday evening in Europe, would set the rate up for a move toward the 1.3445 Fibonacci level. However after Swiss March CPI came in at double the 0.5% forecast, EUR/CHF plummeted to 1.3096 from around 1.3220. It now trades at 1.31073. Jones pegs support at 1.3045 ahead of 1.30 and 1.2965 and still favors the upside while the cross trades above 1.2920. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is a tad lower at 3.0240 vs late Tuesday's level of 3.0250; two traders say \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market at both the 3.0240 and 3.0250 levels. A local trader adds, "if the Singapore dollar is allowed to appreciate, maybe Malaysia will follow suit with the MYR. Our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been tracking the Singapore dollar and will likely continue doing that." He tips a 3.0240-3.0300 range for Thursday, with a 2-month target of 3.0150.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling falls to the day's low against many of its peers after February's industrial output data showed a surprise 1.2% month on month fall against an expected 0.5% increase. GBP/USD drops to 1.6282 from around 1.6340, EUR/GBP climbs to 0.8778 from 0.8757 while GBP/JPY hits 138.23 for a 150sen loss on the day and GBP/CHF is also 150 ticks off its 1.5186 day high. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] HUF strengthens further versus EUR as investors are confident in Hungary's prospective fiscal prudence, one trader says. HUF adds to its gains as markets have already priced in the ECB's likely rate increase, Thursday, which would slightly narrow Hungary's rate advantage versus the euro zone. EUR/HUF is at 263.61 from 264.03 late Tuesday, last time HUF was this strong was April 2010. Raiffeisen Bank trader Matyas Kovacs sees further short-term HUF firming, with EUR/HUF possibly breaking support at 262. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD pares some of its losses late Wednesday, after Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the 29.024 level in the afternoon, say two local traders; the pair is at 29.150 from 29.000 earlier and compared with 29.320 late Friday. "The (suspected) \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were quite mild...I guess the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  just doesn't want the Taiwan dollar to rise too fast, and is not aiming to push it lower," says one of the traders. Another trader adds, "if the stock market continues to be this buoyant, (the pair) could easily fall below 29.00." She tips the pair to hover in 28.95-29.10 range Thursday. The Taiex ended up 1.7% at its highest closing level in nearly two months; foreign investors' net buy totaled NT$22.22 billion. (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 04-06-11 0517ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \pa