\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Central Banks  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Again As Risk Appetite Boosts Local  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jieun Shin  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2011\par 13:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL (Dow Jones)--Central banks in South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesia appeared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday, continuing the fight to slow the rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as better risk appetite bolsters purchases of the region's assets.\par \par \uc2 Big trade surpluses, world-beating economic growth and low interest rates in the U.S. and Europe have pushed a wall of capital into Asia-Pacific economies, driving up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to levels that worry the authorities in many export-reliant economies.\par \par \uc2 Several central banks have responded in recent weeks by selling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars in a bid to tame the rises and keep their economies competitive with those of their neighbors. They're looking over their shoulders at China, where a tightly contained rise in the yuan is putting other Asian exporters at a competitive disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has let the yuan rise 4.4% against the dollar since unpegging the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the greenback in mid-July. In the same period, the Korean won has climbed 11.4%, the Malaysian ringgit 7.8% and the Indonesian rupiah 5.6%.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Thailand were spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, while banks thought to be agents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore have also recently been seen selling the local dollar.\par \par \uc2 On Monday the Bank of Korea sought to weaken the won at a 30-month high, Seoul traders said, estimating the bank bought $500 million around KRW1,085. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped lift the dollar to KRW1,086.60 late in the session, but that was still down from late Friday's KRW1,091.10.\par \par \uc2 "It's like the authorities' warning sign" to keep the dollar well above the KRW1,080 support, said a trader at a local bank trader.\par \par \uc2 The head of the BOK's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market team declined comment.\par \par \uc2 Seoul looks set to dig in. The government has notified several asset-management firms that it is withdrawing some of the funds it had placed with them, apparently to fund more dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Korea Economic Daily said Monday, estimating the total at KRW2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry, like their counterparts elsewhere in Asia, aren't trying to turn the tide in the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just slow their advance.\par \par \uc2 "The weakness of the dollar against the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except the yen, is the main reason for the won's recent strength," said Byeon Ji-young, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Woori Futures. Unless Seoul shares pull back strongly or risk aversion returns and scares global investors off Asian assets, she said, "Players will likely continue to place short orders on the dollar."\par \par \uc2 Similarly, Bank Indonesia appeared to sell the rupiah, buying an estimated $50 million at IDR8,675. But the dollar still fell to IDR8,665 late in the day, vs IDR8,690 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't go all out in defending" the initial level, said a local trader. Aldian Taloputra, an economist at Mandiri Sekuritas, expects the dollar to fall to IDR8,600 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it's not trying to halt the rupiah's rising trend.\par \par \uc2 "We still see room for further appreciation of the rupiah," Bank Indonesia Deputy Gov. Budi Mulya told reporters. "The rupiah appreciation is still in line with our fundamentals. Besides that, all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region are in a strengthening trend, so it's a question of how authorities can maintain stability of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to combat the ringgit's surge, buying dollars at MYR3.0250, traders said. The dollar was around that level late in the day, below Friday's MYR3.0255.\par \par \uc2 -By Jieun Shin, Dow Jones Newswires; 822-3700-1905; jieun.shin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Kanga Kong in Seoul, and I Made Sentana, Andreas Ismar and Farida Husna in Jakarta contributed to this item.) [ 04-04-11 1113GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110404e7440000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
