\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: KLCI Down 0.6%; 1500 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 2,244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.6% at 1514.12 in afternoon trade in very low volume. "Investors are cautious and staying on the sidelines as oil prices rise due to the crisis in the Middle East," a local dealer says. He also says the market is likely to remain subdued for the rest of the week ahead of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rate setting meeting on Friday. He tips 1500 as a strong psychological support in the near term. Market breadth is negative with 512 decliners compared to 146 gainers. Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is down 2.3% at MYR3.36, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Genting Bhd\f28 \uc2 \f2 (3182.KU) is down 1.7% at MYR10.16, Malaysian Airline (3786.KU) is down 1.6% at MYR1.84 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 AMMB Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (1015.KU) is down 1.3% at MYR6.30. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petronas Dagangan\f28 \uc2 \f2  (5681.KU) is up 0.6% at MYR14.28. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The BOK is expected to resume monetary tightening Thursday by raising its policy rate for the second time this year after inflation hit a 27-month high last month amid solid exports and retail sales. Eighteen of 23 economists and fixed-income analysts surveyed by Dow Jones expect the BOK to hike the key rate by 25 bps to 3.00%. Remaining five bet on hold but they expect a second hike to come as early as next month; "we expect the BOK to hike rates this time as inflation is rising and growth remains solid," says Nomura economist Kwon Young-sun. "Indeed, oil prices before the Middle East and North African crisis were mostly global demand-driven and higher inflation expectations were already feeding into higher core inflation. All this suggests that further monetary policy normalization looks warranted." He adds, if oil prices were to soar from the current level, the BOK would be faced with a policy dilemma. Domestic inflation hit a 27-month high of 4.5% in February vs 4.1% in January and above the BOK's target range of 2%-4%. Rate decision is due around 0100 GMT Thursday. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends down 1.2% at 1980.27, ending its two-session rally as foreigners turn net sellers of KRW56 billion worth of stocks; whether the index recovers the key 2000 level this week will likely be a market focus amid lingering concerns over the unrest in Libya, analysts say. "For a while, foreigners are likely to continue flip-flopping from being buyers to sellers," and vice versa, depending on offshore leads, says Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo. Though a massive pullout like the one seen in February is unlikely, foreigners aren't likely to provide any strong upward momentum for Korean stocks either, he adds. Airline company stocks were among the biggest decliners amid rising oil prices; \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) ends down 3.1% at KRW61,600 and \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) is 6.1% lower at KRW9,860. Construction plays were mixed after recent weakness, with \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  (000720.SE) closing up 2.7% at KRW80,300, while Samsung Engineering (028050.SE) falls 4.0% to KRW182,500. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly lower as higher oil prices continue to hurt sentiment, while the resignation of Japan's foreign minister added to Tokyo's gloom; April Nymex crude oil futures rose to their highest level in two and a half years and the contract is trading up $1.66 at $106.08/bbl on Globex. Nikkei is down 1.8%, S&P/ASX is down 1.3%, HSI is 0.3% lower, STI is +0.1% Kospi is down 1.2%, Shanghai Composite is +1.6%, NZX-50 is off 1.4%, Taiex is down 0.7%, Sensex is down 1.8% and SET is +0.5%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD/JPY fell as safe-haven demand intensified amid rising crude oil prices and continued political unrest in the Middle East; the USD/JPY is at 82.21 vs 82.30 late Friday in New York. The EUR/USD is at 1.3972 vs 1.3990 and the EUR/JPY is at 114.89 vs 115.10. Chief Cabinet Secretary Yukio Edano will temporarily replace departing Japanese Foreign Minister Seiji Maehara, NHK and other local media reported. The Japanese government took a EUR1.03 billion stake in bonds issued in January by a European bailout fund, making good on its promise to assist European efforts to calm the region's debt turmoil, a government official said. The total number of job advertisements in Australian newspapers and on the Internet rose 1.2% in seasonally adjusted terms in February from the previous month to an average of 193,555 ads per week. The Australian Industry Group Australian Performance of Construction Index rose 4.4 points to 44.6 in February from January. Spot gold is at $1,437.00/oz, up $4.20 vs NY close. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors will pay attention to ECB President Trichet's speech later in the global day as they look for clues about when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike rates, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, a senior fund manager at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 . "Though the market consensus on the timing of an ECB rate-hike is somewhere around July, some bullish investors are insisting (it will be) April," he says. Other Tokyo dealers shared a similar view, saying they will focus on whether Trichet will use the phase "strong vigilance" which he often uses a month before a rate-hike. The EUR may move sharply depending on his comments, they say. The EUR/JPY is at 114.92 and the EUR/USD is at 1.3973. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0601 GMT [Dow Jones] March Nikkei 225 futures are down 1.8% at 10,490, off their intraday low of 10,460, as a stronger yen and geopolitical uncertainties weigh on the market. USD/JPY is at 82.24, while EUR/JPY is at 114.95. Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga pegs futures' range at 10,350-10,700 for the week. In addition to the effect of high crude prices on the U.S. market, he says conditions for stock market volatility will persist this week unless the Kan administration finds a quick replacement for outgoing Foreign Minister Seiji Maehara following his resignation Sunday. The market is also gearing up for concurrent futures and options settlements at the end of this week, he adds.(judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend gains on the government's reassuring words on inflation control, with coal miners and gold stocks leading gains. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 1.6% at 2989.84, with analysts putting immediate resistance at 3000. "Officials' comments on inflation during the weekend are sending a signal to the market that the country's inflation could be under control, which is prompting investors to chase stocks," says Shi Weixiang, an analyst from Guotai Junan Securities. Among other official comments, China Premier Wen Jiabao pledged Saturday to make combating inflation the government's top priority this year, but signaled an intention to realize the goal without sacrificing relatively fast economic growth and strong job creation. Among leading gainers, coal miners are up on hopes of higher product prices, supported by surging oil prices; \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) and Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) each hit 10% daily upside limit at CNY28.37 and CNY32.56 respectively. Gold miners gain on strength in gold prices; Zijin Mining (601899.SH) is up 2.5% at CNY8.36 and Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) climbs 2.2% to CNY50.38. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 1.1% higher at 1300.76. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.4% at 23,334.43 weighed by U.S. stocks' losses Friday but A-shares' strength (Shanghai Composite is up 1.8%) lends support. Volume is modest at HK$37.25 billion; an intraday low of 23,229.07 may provide immediate support. Daiwa notes China is now the best-performing market in the region year-to-date, with the start of the National People's Congress on March 5 "should re-focus attention on the country's long-term economic-growth prospects and the potential winners from structural reform." Most blue chips are lower led by \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2 's (0293.HK) 3.3% fall to HK$17.70 as Nymex crude now firmly entrenches itself above $100/bbl, and will likely dent airlines' earnings due to higher fuel costs; China Shenhua (1088.HK) is up 3.0% at HK$33.95 while China Coal (1898.HK) rises 2.5% to HK$11.60, tracking their A-share counterparts' rally. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0423 GMT incorrectly stated the day the AFP published the report on the coalition party's withdrawal. The correct version follows:) The Sensex opens lower, trading down 1.5% at 18,199.76, tracking negative cues from some Asian peers. "The political environment of the country has made the market nervous after a coalition member withdrew support from the (federal) government," a local analyst says. The Dravida Munnettra Kazhagam, a regional party from South India, said it had withdrawn from the ruling national coalition as it had been unable to come to an agreement with the Congress party for elections in the state of Tamil Nadu, AFP reported Saturday. The analyst, however, expects the market to bounce back later in the trading session due to value buying in sectors such as banking, capital goods and metals; he tips the Sensex in a 18,150-18,400 range intraday. \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra\uc2  (500520.BY) is down 2.9% at INR656.85, \uc2 Jaiprakash Associates\uc2  (532532.BY) is down 2.5% at INR81.90 and \uc2 Reliance Communications(532712.BY) \uc2 is down 2.4% at INR91.10. \uc2 OnMobile Global\uc2  (532944.BY), which isn't part of the Sensex, is +3.9% at INR215.05 on the company's plan to issue bonus shares. (swagata.gupta@dowjones.com) [ 03-07-11 0143ET ]\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares rise 0.5% to 1001.01 at the midday break; trade is tepid amid a dearth of fresh drivers as gains in energy plays on high oil prices offset weakness in banking stocks. "Uncertainties in political tensions in Libya are an overhang, so investors remain cautious. The (local) market won't go so far," says Assistant Vice President at Phillip Securities Teerada Chanyingyong. She tips resistance at 1008, and support at 995. Among top actives, \uc2 BTS Group Holdings\uc2  (BTS.TH) jumps 7.0% to THB0.76 ahead of the results of a technical evaluation of an auction to operate an extension of an electric rail line in Bangkok, the so-called purple line, expected later; Banpu (BANPU.TH) is up 1.1% at THB722, PTT (PTT.TH) rises 1.8% to THB339, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is up 1.7% at THB44.75 and \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) is up 2.3% at THB182. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end 0.8% lower at 8713.79, led by losses in airline and construction stocks. Alex Huang, an analyst at Mega Securities, tips trading to remain choppy in the near term on lingering concerns on rising oil prices and geopolitical uncertainties in the Middle East. "But as long as oil prices are still below US$120 (a barrel), the stock markets won't fall drastically as it's been largely priced in." He adds, the Taiex may open lower Tuesday, but will probably start to rebound when it hits the 10-day moving average at 8676, which appears to be the index's immediate support, while substantial resistance remains at 8800. Airlines lose ground amid rising fuel costs, with China Airlines (2610.TW) ending 3.7% lower at NT$18.05 and \uc2 EVA Airways\uc2  (2618.TW) off 2.7% at NT$25.25. Builders fall on the government's new tax to curb rising house prices; Advancetek (1442.TW) end down 1.8% at NT$24.80 and \uc2 Goldsun Group\uc2  (2504.TW) is down 2.3% at NT$12.90. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0517 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects the MYR to appreciate further in the medium term. "We expect the current account surplus to remain strong in 2011, while FDI (foreign direct investment) and other investments flows are likely to improve further," it says; the house adds that the balance of payments flows should remain supportive of the MYR. "We expect \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the near term to keep the ringgit stable versus its trading partners. But we think Bank Negara will allow further appreciation by the middle of the year when there are clearer signs of a pick-up in domestic growth momentum," says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . The pair is last at 3.0300 vs 3.0280 late Friday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 03-07-11 0143ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110307e73700040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
