\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Market Debt Wavers In Strength Against Treasurys\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cynthia Lin   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2011\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging-market debt teetered back and forth in its position against U.S. Treasurys on Thursday as economies carefully gauge potential policy moves to be made by their respective governments.\par \par \uc2 The risk premium on the \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Global, or Embig, was one basis point wider Thursday, to 279 basis points over Treasurys. The overall index was up 0.06% for the day.\par \par \uc2 "The market is in a hovering pattern," said Enrique Alvarez, Latin American strategist at IDEAglobal. "There was so much expectation created surrounding the Portuguese refinancing this week and in the end, there was no definitive conclusion coming from that. We're still searching for major price drivers, and we're just not getting them."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury prices rose Thursday, lifted by Federal Reserve purchases and a bit of support following the 30-year bond auction.\par \par \uc2 Going into the year, however, Alvarez said country-specific news may become more of a driver.\par \par \uc2 "There's an underlying trend where the market is beginning to focus more on regional dynamics, where you have a number of different countries entering higher waters related to inflation," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Chile was in focus as participants remained unsure if Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase its benchmark overnight rate by 25 basis points or keep it steady at 3.25% at its monthly monetary policy meeting. Chile's peso ended slightly stronger versus the dollar Thursday at CLP489.50 to the dollar, compared with Wednesday's close of CLP490.20. While talk of inflation continues to grow, on the Embig, Chile expanded two basis points to 116 basis point over Treasurys. Its index gained 0.05% in price terms.\par \par \uc2 Ecuador was a prominent gainer on the Embig, tightening 104 basis points to 788 basis points above Treasurys. Its index gained 1.6% on the day. On Thursday, the country's internal revenue service reported that Ecuador collected $7.86 billion in taxes last year, up 17% from 2009.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso weakened again the dollar following a worse-than-expected reading on U.S. jobless claims. The peso recently traded weaker at MXN12.1145 from Wednesday's MXN12.0855, according to Infosel. Mexican sovereign debt gained on the Embig, tightening two basis points to 147 basis points over Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 "We believe Mexico's economy is stabilizing, which could help the securitization market's recovery," said \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  credit analyst Maria del Sol Gonzalez de Cosio, who anticipates fewer negative ratings for the new assets coming into the market.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Russia's spread gave back tightening that occurred earlier in the day, to remain even on the Embig. On Wednesday, Russian equity indexes made their way to new highs after Prime Minster Vladimir Putin provided official 2010 economic readings, with gross domestic product growth reaching 3.8% and industrial output up by 8.3%.\par \par \uc2 From Asia, Malaysia contributed to the Embig's expansion, with its individual spread wider by four basis points to 113 basis points over Treasurys. Its index was mostly flat on the day. The Malaysian ringgit, like many other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has faced upward pressure against the dollar. Following the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise 25-basis-point rate increase, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pakistan also fed into the wider Embig, with its own spread up 23 basis points to 811 basis points over Treasurys. Its index fell more than 3%. In addition to the mounting political turmoil, Pakistan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began printing new rupee notes at the start of the year even as the nation faces inflationary pressure that has slashed more than 15% of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value in the past year.\par \par \uc2 -By Cynthia Lin, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Anthony Esposito and Laurence Iliff contributed to this article. [ 13-01-11 2110GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110113e71d001h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
