\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Higher Late; 3.0950-3.1200 Range Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2010\par 10:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0827 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1110 vs 3.0890 yesterday; local dealer says short covering pushing pair higher, some corporate flows, but many players staying on sidelines ahead of U.S. non-farm payroll data later today. Near-term trend is for MYR to continue rising; tips 3.0950-3.1200 range Monday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar is just slightly firmer in very quiet pre-payrolls trade Friday with the added risk of this weekend's IMF, World Bank and G7 meetings further dimming the markets appetite to trade. The euro is just a couple of ticks lower at $1.3907, the dollar is unchanged at Y82.38 and the pound is down at $1.5840. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex remains down, last off 0.3% at 20,254.06, vs 20,409.19 opening high, 20,175 intraday low as investors cautious, reluctant to buy after over 14%-rally since Sept. 1. 18 stocks down, 12 up, with Mahindra (500520.BY) top loser - down 3% at INR703.70 on profit-taking. "Trading is subdued. Select blue chips are witnessing some profit-booking, while pharma shares are seeing some buying by overseas funds," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, lack of clear direction from global markets, expensive valuations could keep markets rangebound. Tips Sensex in 20,000-20,550 range, with 2Q earnings starting next Friday when \uc2 Infosys\uc2  begins season, providing future direction. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 2.8% at INR631.60, HDFC (500010.BY) down 1.5% at INR715.80, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) off 1.7% at INR1,107. But, Larsen (500510.BY) +0.6% at INR2,050.60, RIL (500325.BY) +0.6% at INR1,045.15. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS mostly steady as trading quiet on first trading session after 7-day National Day holiday, but long-end tad higher after China's official PMI (released October 1) rises to 53.8 in September from 51.7 in August. 1-year flat at 2.15%-2.22%, 5-year +4 bps at 2.91%-2.99%, 1-to-5-year spread 3 bps wider at 77 bps. Trader at Shanghai-based local bank says no clear direction for swaps in near future as official PMI simply reaffirms recovering trend tipped by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI earlier. Adds, players looking for cues from 3Q GDP (due October 21). (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia August industrial output likely +5.0% on-year vs July's +3.2%, according to median forecast of 11 economists polled by Dow Jones; but economists say broader trend of slowing growth still intact. Joanna Tan, an economist at Forecast in Singapore, says with rising uncertainties about global economic growth, moderation in growth in industrial production likely to continue. Growth in manufacturing component will continue to slow in tandem with exports, which will also weigh on electricity production component, says Affin Investment Bank chief economist Alan Tan. Data expected 0401 GMT Monday.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  cuts its projected GDP growth for Russia this year to 3.5% from 5.3%, while the 2011 estimate is cut to 5.0% from 6.1%. "The spike in food prices and greater uncertainty over our bullish oil price view added more clouds to the growth outlook," broker says. Ruble appreciation has reduced economic growth as Russian imports become more affordable and exports less competitive, Goldman says, while remaining bullish on the near-term prospects for the ruble. Ruble weakens against \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar/euro basket to 35.2457, from 35.0512 late Thursday. Ruble slips to 29.9065 to the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] A very slow start for the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairings Friday with the scary prospects of U.S. payrolls, IMF, World Bank and G7 meetings dimming the market's trading appetite. Overnight the dollar strengthened in late U.S. trade helped by comments from Kansas City Fed President Hoenig and Dallas Fed's Fisher, one suggesting more Fed easing was not needed while the other said it was not a done deal anyway. However, news that \uc2 Moody's\uc2  had put China on review for a possible sovereign upgrade lifted the markets spirits and risk appetite in Asia Friday and the greenback once again came under selling pressure. For Friday U.S. payrolls will dominate with the market likely positioned for a weak number after Wednesday's disappointing ADP employment survey. However, with so much happening over the weekend we may have to wait until Monday to see where the dollar is heading next. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to step in now to cap INR's sharp rise since September. For one, INR's gains reflecting USD's slide, RBI has been of late comfortable allowing local unit to move more in line with global majors. Moreover, real effective exchange rate gains, in fact, have been less for INR vs major export competitors. Rising INR also helps in capping imported inflation and the government also doesn't seem inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this juncture, as Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Thursday. Capital controls as a measure to cap INR also appear highly unlikely at a time of a yawning current account gap. See: =MONEY TALKS: Rising Rupee Won't Force India \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds mostly higher taking cues from U.S. Treasurys overnight; volumes low. Trader says, if U.S. Treasurys continue to rise, trend could continue next week; but players likely cautious ahead of 7-year reopening, budget both due next week. Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to auction MYR3.5 billion ($1.1 billion) of September 2017 MGS Oct. 14. Yields on benchmark MGSs largely down, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.24%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 flat at 3.65%. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe-haven gilts are higher as stocks slip, with traders awaiting US nonfarm payrolls at 1230 GMT. Gilts dropped Thursday as BOE kept its bond purchase program unchanged, although expectations of another round of BOE quantitative easing remain high. "This is likely to keep yields on 2-year gilts moving further below schatz and cause 10-year gilts to outperform even more relative to bunds given this is a more likely location for the BOE's purchases and therefore where the market is likely to focus its pre-emptive attention," says Richard Kelly at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . Ahead of payrolls, UK PPI comes at 0830 GMT. December gilts up 0.30 at 124.99, 10-year gilt up at 115.227 yielding 2.888%.(neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The moderate decline in the Swiss unemployment rate in September should continue through the rest of the year, according to Vontobel. "The adjusted rate has declined steadily since December, and as job vacancies have been buoyant recently, it should continue its downtrend," says economist Ralf Wiedenmann. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish industrial sentiment indicators for August due Friday are seen "solid", says SEB, and will imply "strong recovery in industrial production and new orders". Adds that expected production has been "very high" for the last 2-3 months and expectations are now at an all time high. Sweden's two-year bond yield trades 1.5bps lower at 1.40 while the 10-year also trades unchanged at 2.475.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com) [ 10-08-10 0426ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a80007a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
