s dimming the market's trading appetite. Overnight the dollar strengthened in late U.S. trade helped by comments from Kansas City Fed President Hoenig and Dallas Fed's Fisher, one suggesting more Fed easing was not needed while the other said it was not a done deal anyway. However, news that \uc2 Moody's\uc2  had put China on review for a possible sovereign upgrade lifted the markets spirits and risk appetite in Asia Friday and the greenback once again came under selling pressure. For Friday U.S. payrolls will dominate with the market likely positioned for a weak number after Wednesday's disappointing ADP employment survey. However, with so much happening over the weekend we may have to wait until Monday to see where the dollar is heading next. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to step in now to cap INR's sharp rise since September. For one, INR's gains reflecting USD's slide, RBI has been of late comfortable allowing local unit to move more in line with global majors. Moreover, real effective exchange rate gains, in fact, have been less for INR vs major export competitors. Rising INR also helps in capping imported inflation and the government also doesn't seem inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this juncture, as Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Thursday. Capital controls as a measure to cap INR also appear highly unlikely at a time of a yawning current account gap. See: =MONEY TALKS: Rising Rupee Won't Force India \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds mostly higher taking cues from U.S. Treasurys overnight; volumes low. Trader says, if U.S. Treasurys continue to rise, trend could continue next week; but players likely cautious ahead of 7-year reopening, budget both due next week. Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to auction MYR3.5 billion ($1.1 billion) of September 2017 MGS Oct. 14. Yields on benchmark MGSs largely down, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.24%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.55%, November 2019 flat at 3.65%. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe-haven gilts are higher as stocks slip, with traders awaiting US nonfarm payrolls at 1230 GMT. Gilts dropped Thursday as BOE kept its bond purchase program unchanged, although expectations of another round of BOE quantitative easing remain high. "This is likely to keep yields on 2-year gilts moving further below schatz and cause 10-year gilts to outperform even more relative to bunds given this is a more likely location for the BOE's purchases and therefore where the market is likely to focus its pre-emptive attention," says Richard Kelly at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 . Ahead of payrolls, UK PPI comes at 0830 GMT. December gilts up 0.30 at 124.99, 10-year gilt up at 115.227 yielding 2.888%.(neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The moderate decline in the Swiss unemployment rate in September should continue through the rest of the year, according to Vontobel. "The adjusted rate has declined steadily since December, and as job vacancies have been buoyant recently, it should continue its downtrend," says economist Ralf Wiedenmann. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish industrial sentiment indicators for August due Friday are seen "solid", says SEB, and will imply "strong recovery in industrial production and new orders". Adds that expected production has been "very high" for the last 2-3 months and expectations are now at an all time high. Sweden's two-year bond yield trades 1.5bps lower at 1.40 while the 10-year also trades unchanged at 2.475.(charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0732 GMT [Dow Jones] With help from strong economic numbers and surging commodity prices AUD/USD has been a star performer and is heading toward the holy grail of parity, but no further. See Nick Hastings' \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus for more. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures down 0.3% at 22,907, while cash market hanging on to slim gains (+0.03%) as even strong A-shares market can't offset profit-taking pressure, which building up since September 1, when HSI strings up 21 sessions of gains out of 25 until Thursday. Still, contract trading at quite consistent premium to cash throughout session, suggesting view any HSI pullback due to normal profit-taking. Volume higher than recent sessions at 57,826 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of France business survey points to a business recovery, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  senior economist Dominique Barbet. Notes survey is better in terms of demand and activity across both industry and services, and capacity utilization ratio has fully corrected the August decline. Says the Bank of France's unchanged +0.3% 3Q GDP forecast in the survey is too low; \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  expect +0.4%. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is weaker, but stays within 3.95-3.98 range against the euro. The market awaits U.S. nonfarm payrolls data later Friday, and the zloty is expected to be little changed by the time of publication, \uc2 Bank BPH\uc2  says. Adds, better than expected reading will improve global sentiment, thus PLN is likely to strengthen to below 3.95 against the euro. EUR/PLN now trades at 3.98 from 3.9639 late Thursday, USD/PLN at 2.8585 from 2.8396. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com) [ 10-08-10 0414ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101008e6a800075\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
