\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia PM: Ringgit Level Now Reflects Fundamentals - CNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 186 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 02:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Malaysian Prime Minister Najib Razak said he wasn't worried about the ringgit's strength, CNBC reported Friday, marking a contrast with recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asked if he was comfortable with the ringgit, which is trading around 13-year highs, Najib said,"Yes, we are," according to CNBC, which said the interview was conducted Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "But we are monitoring it in terms of both our fiscal and monetary policy to make sure that the ringgit reflects the fundamentals of the economy," he was quoted as saying. "And at the moment, we believe it does and the stronger ringgit doesn't seem to have a negative impact on our exports."\par \par \uc2 Bank Negara \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has begun to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the ringgit in recent days, after staying out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since April, traders in Kuala Lumpur say. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars last week and as recently as Wednesday at MYR3.1110-MYR3.1120, the traders said.\par \par \uc2 -By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4031 [ 10-09-10 0114GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100910e69a0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
