\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Shares End +0.3%;Resistance Eyed At 1273\b0\par\par\uc2 1,235 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2010\par 10:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0925 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end higher; KLCI +0.3% at 1270.78 led by gains in heavyweights following better-than-expected earnings; construction (+0.7%), property (0.5%). Dealer says Malaysia's stronger-than-expected 4Q GDP growth at 4.5% (vs +3.2% poll forecast, 1.2% decline in 3Q), expectations of more good earnings reports late Wednesday, Thursday may boost stocks tomorrow; tips resistance at 1273, then 1280. Market breadth ends negative; decliners beat advancers 333 to 290 as some lower liners succumb to profit taking. Decliners include Bursa (1818.KU) down 1.2% at MYR7.21, MISC (3816.KU) down 3.6% at MYR7.95. Advancers are CIMB (1023.KU) +2.7% at MYR13.00, BAT (4162.KU) +1.1% at MYR42.08. (KPL)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] Portuguese government bond PGB 3.35% October 2015 is trading at similar asset swap levels versus Irish bond 4.6% April 2016, "though the [Portuguese] auction bond does not stand out as particularly cheap on the domestic curve," says Nordea's senior analyst Jan von Gerich. Adds that in the 10-year sector, meanwhile, Irish bond 4.5% April 2020 is offering some 15 basis points pick-up versus PGB 4.8% June 2020. "We thus find some value in the Portuguese auction bond vs Ireland, even though we generally prefer Irish bonds to Portuguese ones," he says. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] The risks are mounting again for the euro says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . Greece is back in the spotlight with a general strike and the downgrading of its 4 largest banks by Fitch and the ECB seems unconcerned with the euro's recent decline according to comments from executive board member Gonzalez-Paramo. BNP says it now looks for EUR/USD to resume its major downtrend with a break through last week's low of 1.3450 and then target the 1.30 area. EUR/USD now at 1.3516. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] March bunds break through the 124.00 level Wednesday and now test the 124.12 resistance. Demand remains strong after Tuesday's strong rally. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  expects Portugal's EUR1 billion 3.35% October 2015 government bond offer to be easily absorbed due to the small size, it says, but adds that the bond isn't cheap within its curve. Says the bond is trading almost flat to the domestic peer PGB 4.375% June 2014 at +84 basis points in asset swaps. Auction results are due between 1030 GMT and 1045 GMT. (BEM)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan average interbank overnight rate steady at 1-month high 0.106% vs yesterday close, indicating banks short of available cash as people save money at banks' branches after week-long holidays, while cash in branches not immediately available for banks, says local bank trader. Says she doesn't expect situation to improve until end of month. Tips rate to sustain at around 0.105% for rest of month. (WUM)\par \par \uc2 0904 GMT [Dow Jones] Swiss banks still have a lot of overseas assets to sell, and a lot of Swiss francs to repatriate, which means the SNB "will remain active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Swiss banks' overseas assets have shrunk by over CHF1 trillion since the credit crunch began, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. EUR/CHF trades heavily at 1.4641. (KJM)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 12 rises to 150 bps vs Tuesday's 141 bps as multimillion vehicle recall by \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . (7203.TO) darkening outlook for Japan's export-driven economy, prompting investors to buy back protection against bankruptcy risks, says Mizuho Securities credit analyst Seiichiro Matsumoto. Notes many players focused on remarks from Toyota's \uc2 president Akio Toyoda\uc2 , who's slated to appear before Congressional committee later in day; "If his comments raise criticism about Toyota, concerns may grow that sales of Japanese products could decrease overseas, which will choke off the current economic recovery." CDS on Toyota traded at 100 bps, sharply up from 86 bps on Monday. Other single-name CDS deals Wednesday include \uc2 Mitsui & Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8031.TO) at 75 bps, unchanged from Feb. 5; \uc2 Acom Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8572.TO) at 340 bps vs 405 bps on Dec. 25; Japan's USD-denominated sovereign CDS at 75 bps vs 71 on Tuesday. (TTA)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is almost flat as USD retreating against EUR is balanced with negative impact of ongoing domestic political tensions stemming from military coup allegations. USD/TRY trades at 1.5415 from 1.5410 Tuesday. Meanwhile the benchmark November 16, 2011 bond trades at 8.94% from 8.91% Tuesday. Analysts forecast TRY to trade between 1.5300 and 1.5500 and the benchmark bond to trade between 8.9% and 9%, in the short term. (IHA)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] BOT seeking proper timing to begin tightening monetary policy, but no immediate sign of inflationary pressure in economy, Governor Tarisa tells Dow Jones Newswires, suggesting BOT in no rush to hike rates. "Core inflation is still very much within our range" of 0.5%-3.0%, "so the pressure is not there yet," Tarisa says; adds Thailand's economic recovery broad-based, with improvement in exports, imports, domestic consumption. But concerns about global recovery, Euro-zone sovereign debt remain, could threaten Thailand's growth. Says THB expected to be more volatile this year due to anticipated swings in capital flows, but says BOT will stand firm on policy to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb excessive volatility. BOT may also further relax regulations (although not substantially) to facilitate investment outflows after easing rules earlier this year in move that could help reduce need for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (LHM,PPP)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  4Q GDP +4.5% vs +3.2% median forecast of 15 economists in Dow Jones poll, rebounds from 3Q contraction of 1.2%. 2009 GDP contracts 1.7% vs 3.0% decline forecast by government. PM Najib Razak says growth in 4Q due to stronger private consumption, boosted by government's stimulus package; says targetting growth of 5.0% in 2010 vs current official forecast of +2.0% to +3.0%. Director of Action Economics David Cohen says numbers stronger than expected but "in line with the pattern of better numbers among Asian economies"; adds, 5.0% target this year "achievable even if assuming a moderate trajectory." Cohen says stronger growth numbers give \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to raise rates "but we think (Bank Negara) could be patient... with inflation still contained there is no compelling reason to be the first in the region to raise rates." Next policy rate meeting on March 4. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] Keep an eye on the gilt-bund spread given the UK has a 10-year auction Wednesday, says RBS's Melinda Burgess. While the bank's strategists expect the auction to go well, she remains less convinced on the success of future auctions, and says this will be a risk for sterling. So she favors selling GBP/USD, which currently trades at 1.5454. The 10-year gilt/bund spread is currently 98 bps. (GST) [ 02-24-10 0425ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100224e62o0006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
