\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Shares End Flat; 1230-1260 Range Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,257 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2009\par 11:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end flat with benchmark KLCI +0.02 point at 1,246.86. Dealer says volume heavy at 1.35 billion shares traded, lower liners, high beta stocks attracting most interest; morning gains (intraday high of 1,256.59) give way to profit-taking in afternoon session. Market breadth negative with 413 decliners vs 332 advancers. "Although the trendline is still positive, the gains over the past few days are starting to look a little shaky...there could be a period of consolidation before the market advances further," says dealer, adding pre-weekend profit taking might weigh index tomorrow, tips 1,230-1,260 range. CIMB (1023.KU) down 1.1% at MYR12.18, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.9% at MYR7.32, MAS (3786.KU) down 2.0% at MYR3.00. DiGi (6947.KU) +4.3% at MYR22.40, \f28 \uc2 \f2 KL Kepong\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2445.KU) +2.0% at MYR14.48, Bursa (1818.KU) +3.2% at MYR8.45. (KPL)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HeidelbergCement\uc2 's (HEI.XE) new five-year bonds are now bid at par, while the seven-year notes are bid at 99.875 Thursday, according to syndicate bankers. The issuer Wednesday offered a EUR1B 5-year note at 98.465, and a EUR1B 7-year tranche at 97.349, alongside a EUR500M 10-year note priced at 96.739. (SYC)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR ends tad lower at 9,330 vs 9,375 Wednesday close, lowest finish since September 24 last year. Pair hits 9,290 intraday but Bank Indonesia suspected of propping up USD, buying about $50 million around that level, dealers say. Broad USD weakness, buying interest in local assets boosts IDR; "the pair still looks like it will head down further tomorrow," one dealer says. Dealers tip 9,250-9,350 range Friday. (RLC)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD closed above its September high of 1.4865, and is now on target for its long-term Fibonacci level of 1.5225, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones. Trading at 1.4940, she says only below the 20-day moving average at 1.4715 would indicate a near term corrective dip towards the 55-day moving average at 1.4482 is underway. (GST)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] The HUF strengthens further against the EUR, breaking the psychological level of 270, on the back of a continuing stock market rally and due to investor confidence getting firmer in Hungary's fiscal credibility, an analyst says. The cross is at 266.90, versus 267.10 Wednesday evening, with the next support level at 266. "The next support level will be a hard one to break, it may break if the DJIA increases above 13000," the analyst says. (VGU)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP ends lower on prevailing USD weakness against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair ends at 13-month low 46.33 vs previous close 46.45, volume heavy; "The dollar is weak across the board and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to let go a few centavos," says local bank trader; tips PHP may continue to advance Friday, next support 46.25, resistance at 46.50. (RSA)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares trade higher on gains in oil stocks with benchmark KSE-100 index +0.7% at 9,877.18. "There is fresh buying in oil stocks as sentiment was boosted after the announcement from Pakistan Oilfields that it found deposits of gas and oil in partnership with other oil companies," says dealer; but adds, overall sentiment is cautious with volumes on the lower side because of the terrorist attack in the eastern city of Lahore, where deaths rose to 26 including 16 policemen; says any more incidents in coming sessions to cast negative shadow over equity markets. However, a local analyst says, "Selling pressure will be absorbed as the government is expected to get $1.5 billion from Kerry Lugar bill approval with some of the objectionable conditions getting amended soon." Oil and Gas Development +2.1% at PKR113.50, Pakistan Oilfields +2.3% at PKR230.51. (HAZ)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan CDS spreads narrow slightly with stocks higher (Nikkei +1.8% at 10,238.65); iTraxx Japan Series 12 trades at 115, 119, 117 bps vs yesterday's 120. But index may not move into genuine tightening phase yet, as major credit events, such as concerns over \uc2 Japan Airlines\uc2 ' (9205.TO) revival plan, remain; JAL shares down sharply today, CDS levels widen on indication basis. No trade for JAL CDS today; indicated at 2500 bps. Single-name CDS deals include \uc2 Acom Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8572.TO) at 550 bps vs 725 bps on Oct. 8, \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . (7203.TO) at 60 bps vs 65 bps on Oct. 13, \uc2 Itochu Corp\uc2 . (8001.TO) at 120 bps vs 100 bps on July 31. \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp\uc2 .'s dollar-subordinated CDS at 85 bps, unchanged from last trade on Sept. 15.(MEF)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PKR ends lower at 83.25 vs 83.32 last close on exporter selling; dealer at local bank tips pair in 83.20-83.25 band Friday. "Importer demand was met quite comfortably and with inflows expected from foreign portfolio investors and exporters in coming days, dollar may fall further," dealer adds. (HAZ)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys fall in London Thursday as optimism grows ahead of 3Q results from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS) and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) following Wednesday's strong earnings reports. On the data front, September US CPI, forecast +0.1%, and October NY Fed Empire State Survey and US jobless claims for week ending October 10, forecast at 515k, are due at 1230 GMT. The Treasury yield curve moves marginally steeper, with the 2-year/10-year benchmark spread widening to +251.2bp from Wednesday's +250.2bp. The 2-year note is down 2/32 at 100 3+/32, yielding 0.939%, the 10-year down 11/32 at 101 13/32, yielding 3.455%, with the 30-year bond down 25/32 at 103 4+/32, yielding 4.311%. Bunds and gilts move lower ahead of supply. December bunds are down 0.56 at 121.44, with December gilts down 0.44 at 119.09. (NKA)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.3590 but off intraday low of 3.3450 vs yesterday's close at 3.3680; dealer with local brokerage says pair tracking USD/Asians, intense USD selling over past few days curbed somewhat by concerns Asian central bankers may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively to stop greenback's slide. "Even though (\b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not spotted, these are definite concerns in the market...it has given some players the excuse to take profit," says dealer; adds, 3.3500 support likely to be re-tested tomorrow, 3.3800 resistance seen. (KPL)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish zloty is trading slightly higher as global sentiment remains upbeat, pushing aside the effect of canceled privatization of power company Enea, which implies there will be no boost for the zloty from foreign flows, local analyst says. EUR/PLN at 4.1960 from 4.1980 Wed; USD/PLN 2.8092 from 2.8168. Treasuries mostly 2-year paper flat at 5.02%; 5-year at 5.67% from 5.65%; 10-year unchanged at 6.16%. (MES) [ 10-15-09 0522ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020091015e5af0006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
