\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA: Asian Shares Drop,  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Sharply Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carlos Tejada   \par \uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2008\par 10:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    OF THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Asian stocks were sold off heavily Thursday, knocking many of the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sharply lower and prompting governments to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 Markets in Tokyo, Hong Kong and South Korea fell in response to a broad selloff overnight in the U.S. Tokyo's benchmark index fell 6.9%, while Hong Kong and Seoul were down 4% and 6.7% respectively. Australia's major benchmark dropped 4.2% and Shanghai fell 1.7%. Mumbai and Singapore were down 4.1% and 2.8% late Thursday. Jakarta was down 2.8% late.\par \par \uc2 The selling continued in Europe, where the DJ Stoxx 600 index was 3% lower. U.S. stock futures were down 1% to 1.5% in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 The stock hits reflect fears of deflation in the U.S., a major market for Asia-made goods, and the impact of a slowdown on commodity-rich nations like Australia and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 That has led to a flight to safer investments typically denominated in U.S. dollars and the Japanese yen, taking money out of Asian markets and contributing to a drop in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's part of the wholesale destruction of wealth across the planet," said Patrick Bennett, a strategist with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in Hong Kong. "With asset and equity prices tumbling, investors are being encouraged to cut exposure completely."\par \par \uc2 Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can boost big exporting nations because it makes their goods cheaper to sell in other markets. But declining \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit countries with extensive foreign borrowing needs, can drive up inflation by making imports more expensive and can scare off foreign investment.\par \par \uc2 Among the hardest hit, Indonesia's rupiah and South Korea's won fell to their lowest level against the U.S. dollar since the Asian financial crisis a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's baht fell to its weakest level since March 2007, while Taiwan's dollar hit a three-week low and the New Zealand and Australian dollars also weakened further.\par \par \uc2 The central banks of South Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Philippines, Indonesia, Singapore and India \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , local traders said. Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued data that showed it plowed A$3.15 billion into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last month to defend the Australian dollar against a sharp decline.\par \par \uc2 Japan and Hong Kong have defied the trend, the former because its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as a safe haven and the latter because it is pegged to the value of the appreciating U.S. dollar. The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , put roughly HK$15 billion into the market Thursday to bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value within its trading band with the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Economists cautioned that the current environment hasn't reached the point of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis, when domestic funds followed foreign investors as they ran for the exits. Most governments have moved to back bank deposits to reassure consumers. Regional financial institutions have cleaner balance sheets than a decade ago, and bad loans have so far had only a minor effect on regional banks.\par \par \uc2 Still, continuing hits to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue could erode consumer confidence, leading locals to consider moving their money elsewhere. "The impact on confidence could become a much bigger problem," said Sim Moh Siong, an economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in Singapore. "If this goes beyond how much money foreigners remove from Asia, the situation could broaden out."\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the Nikkei Stock Average of 225 companies ended down 6.9% at 7703.04. Shares of major banks and brokerages were hammered, with \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\uc2  tumbling 10% to Y281,500 and \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  ending down 11% at Y666.\par \par \uc2 Shares of nonlife insurers dropped after sector companies reported dismal fiscal first half results Wednesday and sharply reduced their earnings forecasts for the full fiscal year ending March.\par \par \uc2 Although most of the bad news had already been factored in by investors, forecast revisions confirmed the grim outlook. \uc2 Tokio Marine Holdings\uc2  went limit-down at Y2,220, and \uc2 Mitsui Sumitomo Insurance Group Holdings\uc2  also went limit-down at Y2,035.\par \par \uc2 Real estate stocks also sank, dogged by credit concerns, with \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  skidding 14% to Y1,070, and Mitsubishi Estate falling 13% to Y1,077.\par \par \uc2 In Seoul, the Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, finished down 68.13 points, or 6.7%, at 948.69, based on skepticism over conditions in the U.S., particularly among financials.\par \par \uc2 Shinhan Financial Group\uc2  declined 9.7% to KRW26,400, and KB Financial Group plunged by the daily limit of 15% to KRW24,550. Among builders, \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  and GS Engineering & Construction both dropped by the daily 15% limit to KRW42,100 and KRW45,200, respectively.\par \par \uc2 In Shanghai, the benchmark Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares, ended down 1.7%, or 33.71 points, at 1983.76. Railway companies and insurance companies fell on profit-taking.\par \par \uc2 China Railway Group Ltd\uc2 . dropped 4.1% to CNY5.82 after gaining 4.7% Wednesday, while \uc2 China Life Insurance Co\uc2 . fell 4.0% to CNY20.24 after rising 6.1% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 -By Carlos Tejada, The Wall Street Journal; carlos. tejada@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (Takashi Mochizuki and Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo and Rosalind Mathieson in Singapore also contributed to this article.) [ 20-11-08 0946GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081120e4bk000ff\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
