\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Southeast Asia News Highlights 0900 GMT Nov 19\b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2008\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR - Pirates have hijacked a Thai fishing boat with 16 crew members on board off the Somali coast, said Noel Choong, an official with the Kuala Lumpur-based International Maritime Bureau.\par \par \uc2 Piracy in the Gulf of Aden, one of the world's busiest sea routes, has surged this year and last weekend pirates captured a Saudi supertanker carrying $100 million of oil, the largest seizure ever.\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 BEIJING - China will start building oil and gas pipelines through Myanmar next year, enabling it to slash the journey time for crude oil imports from the Middle East, state media said, citing an official.\par \par \uc2 The pipelines into China's southwestern Yunnan province would cut out oil cargoes' long detour through the congested Malacca Strait as well as strengthen China's access to rich energy reserves in Myanmar itself.\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - \uc2 Neptune Orient Lines\uc2 , the world's seventh largest container carrier, said it will shed 9 percent of its workforce and warned the outlook was grim as the shipping sector faces up to a prolonged downturn.\par \par \uc2 NOL shares fell almost 3 percent after the firm said in a statement it will cut 1,000 jobs, mostly in North America, as it battles to weather a global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 TAIPEI - Indonesia's rupiah dipped to a fresh 7-year low, while the South Korean won and the Taiwan dollar hovered at three-week troughs as concerns over a deepening global downturn and capital outflows hit Asian markets.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah fell 2.6 percent from the previous day's close to hit a low of 12,060 to the U.S. dollar during Wednesday trade, with the market watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves after it said it would not allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hit "irrational" levels.\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK - Asian rice prices rose and were expected to stay firm over the next few weeks, supported by a Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheme and fresh demand from Iraq and \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Vietnamese rice, exporters and traders said.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's benchmark 100 percent B grade white rice rose 9 percent from last week to $580 per tonne as a government buying programme kicked in, exporters said. (Compiled by Carl Bagh; Editing by Jerry Norton)\par \par \uc2 SOUTHEAST ASIA-HIGHLIGHTS 0900 GMT|LANGEN|Table|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|G|RBN|SI|IF|PHD|RNP|DNP|PGE|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081119e4bj000mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
