\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia falls on inflation, c.banks move to support\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melissa Chia   \par \uc2 782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2008\par 05:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 5 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weighed down by growing concerns about inflation on Thursday, as the Philippine peso fell to a 7-month low and the Thai baht hit its weakest since February against a broadly stronger dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> fell below a psychologically significant level of 44-per-dollar to 44.10 in early trading on Thursday, marking its weakest level since late October.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Manila said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars to support the peso, which fell after the country's annual inflation in May rose to a nine-year high of 9.6 percent, above market expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Higher inflation is pressuring the peso exchange rate right now, and it is also pressuring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address the inflation pressures," a trader in Manila said.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is due to review its interest rate policy on Thursday, and economists polled were evenly divided over whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise rates or leave them on hold. [ID:nMAN151776]\par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=TH> fell 0.9 percent to a near 4-month low of 32.885 per dollar, while the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> fell by a similar margin to 3.2660 to the dollar, its lowest since mid-May.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Kuala Lumpur said they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 3.2610.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and India separately announced fuel price increases on Wednesday in a move to reduce subsidies, which fanned fears of the inflationary impact on economic growth in these countries and contributed selling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Suffice to say I think inflation risks are on the upside," said Barclay's regional economist Wai Ho Leong, referring to the Malaysian economy.\par \par \uc2 "Clearly households might be feeling the pinch, because on one hand we don't really see the tangible relief measures being doled out in a systematic way," he said.\par \par \uc2 In light of the price reforms, economists expected Malaysia's inflation in 2008 to rise to a 10-year high of 4.7 percent, and foresee a rise in the benchmark interest rate by 50 basis points to 4 percent by the end of the year, a Reuters poll indicated. [ID:nSP162272]\par \par \uc2 GROWING INFLATION CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> fell by a fifth of a percent to 9,329 per dollar. The Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised it key interest rate by 25 basis points to 8.5 percent, trumping earlier expectations for a 50-basis-point rate hike.\par \par \uc2 Analysts warned that other countries in Asia might have to follow Indonesia's lead in raising interest rates as commodity and food prices continue to push inflation higher in the region. [ID:nBNG169466]\par \par \uc2 The stronger dollar on Thursday, which hit a three-month high against the yen, also pressured Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against the euro <EUR=> and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , extending its gains made the previous day after the Federal Reserve chief Ben Bernanke emphasised inflation concerns, reinforcing views the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is done with cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 Both the Singapore dollar <SGD=> and the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP>, two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia which investors are bullish about, fell by 0.4 percent each, with the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inching down to a 2-1/2-week low of 1.3710 per U.S. dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0817 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.13         104.65       -1.39\par  Sing dlr        1.3681         1.3651       -0.22\par  Taiwan dlr      30.375         30.350       -0.08\par  Korean won     1023.00        1018.00       -0.49\par  Baht             32.88          32.60       -0.85\par  Peso             44.04          43.95       -0.22\par  Rupiah         9323.00        9310.00       -0.14\par  Rupee            42.85          42.77       -0.19\par  Ringgit         3.2585         3.2390       -0.60\par  Yuan            6.9438         6.9390       -0.07\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.13         111.33       +4.90\par  Sing dlr        1.3681         1.4382       +5.12\par  Taiwan dlr      30.375         32.443       +6.81\par  Korean won     1023.00         935.70       -8.53\par  Baht             32.88          33.68       +2.43\par  Peso             44.04          41.28       -6.27\par  Rupiah         9323.00        9390.00       +0.72\par  Rupee            42.85          39.41       -8.03\par  Ringgit         3.2585         3.3050       +1.43\par  Yuan            6.9438         7.3041       +5.19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Neil Fullick)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DN