\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asia Stocks Down,  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Up; Oil In Focus   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Februar 2008\par 05:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stocks remain lower Friday in the wake of a retreat on Wall Street, and as a new record for crude oil and stronger regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  add to broad economic concerns.   \par \par \uc2 "There's very little positive news to hang your hat on today," said Patrick Crabb, senior institutional trader at  \uc2 Goldman Sachs JB Were\uc2  in Sydney.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the risk-averse mood, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mostly higher. The Japanese yen has caught its usual safe haven bid, a factor that is weighing on shares of Japanese exporters.   \par \par \uc2 Staying near multi-year highs are the Malaysian ringgit, Singapore dollar and Taiwan dollar, reflecting the widespread U.S. dollar bearishness now pervading markets.   \par \par \uc2 Central banks in the region continue to tolerate the moves, in a bid to counter the inflationary pressures many economies are facing due to lofty energy and commodity prices, with their absence from the market on Friday being noted by traders who expect local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rising.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are largely flat in screen trade but spreadbetting firms are calling European stocks to open mildly lower.   \par \par \uc2 In Asia, stocks in Japan are faring the worst, with the Nikkei 225 down 2.6% to 13,558.19. With the yen strengthening, exporters are faring particularly poorly.  \uc2 Nissan Motor Co.\uc2  (7201.TO) has fallen 3% to Y961 while  \uc2 Sony Corp.\uc2  (6758.TO) is off 4% to Y5010, and  \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) down 3.2% at Y5,720.   \par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is down 1.9% at 5518.20 after touching a 5-day low of 5515.60, amid worries about the banking sector after the chief financial officer of the country's second largest bank by market capitalization,  \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  (CBA.AU), told a local newspaper that the bank is "feeling enormous pain" from funding costs. Shares of CBA are down 5% to A$41.85, while the S&P/ASX Financials subindex is off 3.7% to 5,183.   \par \par \uc2 Despite surging commodity prices, resource stocks in Australia are offering the broad market little support, with the materials and metals and mining subindexes each only up about 0.1%.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Korea's Kospi Composite is down 1.3% to 1,713.78 while Singapore's Straits Times Index is 1.9% lower at 3,017, and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index is down 1.5% to 24,206.96. In India the Sensex is off about 0.6% to 17,709.64 as traders await the country's budget, due later Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The stock declines follow a retreat on Wall Street amid concerns about U.S. banks, consumers and the impact of rising oil prices. Fueling those concerns, Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke, during a second day of congressional testimony Thursday, noted that small banks could yet fail because of the housing crunch.   \par \par \uc2 And traders, who fully expect the Federal Reserve to cut interest rates by half a percent at its mid-March meeting, are now wondering if the cut might be as large as 75 basis points. Fed funds futures contracts are pricing in a 28% chance of a cut of that size.   \par \par \uc2 Those expectations have weighed on the dollar, driving it to multi-year lows against several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar also hit a record low against the Swiss franc; recently it was trading around CHF1.0504 after falling to CHF1.0476.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit its lowest level against the yen since May 2005, and was recently around Y104.74. The Euro is around Y159.04.   \par \par \uc2 Among other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. dollar has fallen to a 10.5-year low of THB31.93, and is trading at 10-year lows of MYR3.1910 against the Malaysian ringgit. "We are seeing strong foreign fund buying of the ringgit, but no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as yet," a trader in \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar meanwhile has fallen to another 35-month low of NT$30.755.   \par \par \uc2 Mansoor Mohi-uddin, strategist at  \uc2 UBS\uc2  thinks the dollar will settle down in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 "Euro shorts will be forced to cover. Secondly, oil prices have made record highs, increasing the inflows into dollar-diversifying sovereign wealth funds. Thirdly, the cyclical picture has diverged sharply over the last couple of weeks between America and Europe."   \par \par \uc2 Still, dollar bulls shouldn't despair entirely, he thinks, with the dollar set for a medium-term rebound as foreign central banks are likely to be forced to cut rates. "Short-term dollar weakness now will give better levels to buy later this year."   \par \par \uc2 The dollars retreat may be contributing to gains in commodity prices, traders say. Crude oil crossed above $103 a barrel to touch a record high of $103.05 in electronic trading, before pulling back. Most recently crude was closer to $102.58, down a penny from the close of regular trading in New York.   \par \par \uc2 Spot gold is also robust, quoted up 45 cents from the New York close, at $969.95 per troy ounce, and with some expecting it to test $1,000 before long.   \par \par \uc2 But the weakening dollar may not be the biggest driver of a recent surge in commodity prices, analysts at  \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  note.   \par \par \uc2 The "current emphasis on exchange rates risks diverting attention from both the extent of commodity price increases and the key drivers of recent moves," they wrote to clients. Rather, "supply losses, strong demand and low inventory levels are the key drivers."   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, falling stocks have boosted government bond prices. Futures on Japan Government bonds were recently up 0.59 to 138.28, with the yield on 10-year bonds down five basis points to 1.375%.   \par \par \uc2 Also, the yield on 2-year Treasury notes was earlier quoted below 1.75%, the lowest point since April 2004, with the spread between the 2- and 10-year notes pushing out to 187.55 basis points, its widest since July 2004. Recently the 2-year yield was around 1.77%.   \par \par \uc2 Asian credit default swaps have also widened with the Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment grade index quoted at 162-172 basis points compared with 149-152 basis points Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson; Dow Jones Newswires; rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com; +65-6415-4141.   \par \par \uc2 -By Mohammed Hadi; Dow Jones Newswires; mohammed.hadi@dowjones.com; +65-6415-4147.   \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 29-02-08 0458GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080229e42t0004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
