\par\par \b \uc2 MONEY TALKS:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Currency \highlight\uc2  Angst On The Rise In Asia   \b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2008\par 01:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Rosalind Mathieson   \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian central banks have been getting a bit twitchy as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to rise, but that doesn't mean they will try and put an end to it.   \par \par \uc2 There may be a pickup in spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rhetoric or even administrative measures (as evidenced by Taiwan's actions of late).   \par \par \uc2 This, though, will be aimed at slowing the appreciation in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than stopping it altogether.   \par \par \uc2 Governments tend to prefer weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the competitive advantage this provides to exporters.   \par \par \uc2 Central banks aren't as one-sided, however. Especially in times like these, when they are faced with a slower economic growth outlook but also rising price pressures.   \par \par \uc2 Letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gain - in a managed fashion - can help take some of the steam out of inflation (though only to some extent, it can't really do much about domestically-originating inflation pressures).   \par \par \uc2 So the question becomes one of relativity: Asian central banks are likely to accept some appreciation in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but don't want people trying to capitalize on that by pushing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up in wanton fashion.   \par \par \uc2 The central banks have therefore been making their presence felt of late in several countries, in particular Singapore, Taiwan and Malaysia, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been especially strong.   \par \par \uc2 There has been suspected spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Singapore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Taiwan, to buy the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 There have also been moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Taiwan to curtail speculation in the onshore non-deliverable forwards market, including by exporters who reportedly have been straying from using plain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward contracts.   \par \par \uc2 But there hasn't been the sort of barrage of rhetoric and large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would accompany a bid to halt a trend.   \par \par \uc2 In some cases, authorities have even cited the higher \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a sign of economic strength, i.e. of resilience in the face of the offshore headwinds coming from the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 And none of the central banks are likely to be silly enough to do something like slap on capital controls. They tend to not work well, for one thing.   \par \par \uc2 In the medium term, anyway, Malaysia, Taiwan and Singapore may find their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  do some deflating on their own.   \par \par \uc2 Some of the momentum in the first two countries right now is tied up with coming elections.   \par \par \uc2 In Malaysia, that has led to some fairly large spending promises for infrastructure and other developments aimed at luring in foreign investors. Some of those pledges though may turn out to be little more that hot air, once the elections are out of the way.   \par \par \uc2 In Taiwan, the hype is all about the potential for a change in government, and for that new government to warm up the relationship with Beijing.   \par \par \uc2 That has led to a level of excitement about the potential for rapid progress in allowing investment between China and Taiwan and greater tourism. But those expectations are also at risk of disappointment - any thawing is likely to come pretty slowly, and the doors aren't going to be swung wide open to a flood of tourists from the mainland, all armed with cash and credit cards to burn.   \par \par \uc2 For Singapore, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the main policy management tool, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has been well-concerned about inflation, so authorities would think some local dollar strength is warranted. But the relatively small and export-dependent economy there is heading for a slowdown, and that may yet help bring rents and other costs down.   \par \par \uc2 Look for continued action from central banks to try and keep a lid on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But unless it turns into a large speculative attack, the action should remain relatively modest.   \par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4141, rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Rosalind Mathieson is the Managing Editor for Dow Jones Markets News in Asia. She has 13 years of experience as a political and financial reporter and editor, having worked previously at Australian Associated Press in Sydney and Canberra.)   \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-02-08 0022GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1171 70779   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080221e42l00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\p