\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  weaker despite stocks rally   \b0\par\par\uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2007\par 04:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 23 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on Tuesday, yielding ground to a recovering U.S. dollar and failing to find any support from a rally in stock markets as traders groped for direction.   \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit led the decline in the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rebounded sharply from its record lows struck against the euro <EUR=> on Monday after what traders interpreted as G7 indifference towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness. It also climbed back against the yen <JPY=> and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  But trading was marked by confusion, with some looking for bargains in the oversold dollar against the Asians and others returning to risky assets. Some traders also felt the dollar's decline had been overdone -- its trade-weighted basket <.DXY> has fallen nearly 2 percent in two weeks.   \par \par \uc2  In Asia, stocks rebounded. MSCI's measure of Asia Pacific stocks outside Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> climbed to 554 from Monday's lows near 540. The index had its steepest fall since Aug. 16 on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  At its weakest, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> shed 0.4 percent to hit 1.4678 per U.S. dollar, the ringgit <MYR=> lost 0.3 percent to hit 3.38 per dollar and the high-yielding Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> dropped a quarter of a percent to 9,135 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar subsequently rallied to 1.4650, with traders preparing for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate in anticipation of tighter monetary policy as inflation accelerates.   \par \par \uc2  "There's marginal downward pressure on U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar due to inflationary pressures," said Standard Chartered Bank's strategist Thomas Harr.   \par \par \uc2  "We are in strong end of band already. I see the pair at 1.46 by year-end."   \par \par \uc2  Singapore's annual inflation in September was 2.7 percent, softening from a 12-year high hit in August of 2.9 percent. Still, high oil and house prices are expected to keep inflation levels elevated.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come off a 10-year high of 1.4577 hit last week.   \par \par \uc2  But it has still risen 2.6 percent in the past month, a rally sparked by the Fed's 50-basis-point rate cut on Sept. 18 and positioning for Singapore's policy review on Oct. 10, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) increased the pace of appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  COPYCAT RINGGIT   \par \par \uc2  The ringgit has so far dropped half a percent in two sessions. Traders said the ringgit and Singapore dollar were moving in tandem, both undermined by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by their respective central banks after hitting decade highs last week.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said they would not buy the ringgit against dollars unless there was a big rise in the Singapore dollar as well.   \par \par \uc2  "After all dollar/ringgit is negative carry if you sell dollars, so Bank Negara can always afford to buy dollars," a trader in Hong Kong said, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Malaysia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3.5 percent overnight policy rate is below the fed funds rate at 4.75 percent, making it more profitable to hold dollars.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts at  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  said that in contrast with the Singapore dollar, which was on the strong side of its trade-weighted band, the ringgit was trading about 180 basis points on the weaker side of an estimated informal trade-weighted policy band.   \par \par \uc2  They also expect Malaysian inflation data due on Wednesday to show some upward pressure on consumer prices.   \par \par \uc2  "Still, the challenge remains that the ringgit is sensitive to risk appetite and the vagaries of equity market sentiment. In this respect, we expect long ringgit positions to experience some downside and volatility this week,"  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Hong Kong dollar <HKD=> tested the upper end of its 7.75-7.85 linked exchange rate system to the U.S. dollar, driven by cash flows into the stock market and speculation that the peg might either be abandoned or recalibrated. [nSHA15826].   \par \par \uc2  And the Indian rupee <INR=IN> bucked the weakness in the region, buoyed by the view that foreigners would eventually return to the stock market despite last week's curbs on some derivative investment vehicles. [nBOM232170].  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   Change on the day at 0821 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.51         114.03       -0.42\par  Sing dlr        1.4650         1.4613       -0.25\par  Taiwan dlr      32.580         32.570       -0.03\par  Korean won      917.90         916.90       -0.11\par  Peso             44.06          44.05       -0.02\par  Rupiah         9135.00        9115.00       -0.22\par  Rupee            39.66          39.89       +0.59\par  Ringgit         3.3730         3.3685       -0.13\par  Yuan            7.5062         7.5098       +0.05\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen